window.REAPER_NOTEBOOK = ["Start Date: 2025/5/29\nTaipei, Taiwan\n2025-2026 Push Back\n", "Notebook \nIntroduction \nPreface\nHello, we are Wego Robotics.\nThis season marks the third year of our VEX Robotics \nprogram since our club was founded. In addition to \ndocumenting our progress for reference and for judges’ \nreview, this year’s engineering notebook also serves a \nsecond purpose — to act as a guide for future members \nof Wego Robotics.\nWe aim for this notebook to be more detailed and \neducational than before, explaining not only what we built \nand programmed, but also how our team operates, \norganizes, and learns. We hope that future members can \nuse this record to understand our methods, improve upon \nthem, and continue building a stronger club in the years \nto come.\n2", "How to Read This Notebook\nThis notebook is organized into nine major parts, \nfollowing the same order as our real development process \n— from the people behind the robot, to the robot itself, \nand ﬁnally, to the lessons we want to pass on.\nEach part within itself is organized chronologically, but \ncan also stand alone as a complete record of that topic.\n3", "Table of Contents\n1 TEAM\nBRAINSTORMING3\nROBOT 2.1\n- T eam members\n- T eam organization\nGAME ANAL YSIS2\n- Game rules and Scoring\n- Scoring Observations\n- Skills\n- Updates\n- Basic Structure Brainstorm\n- Chassis\n- Intake\n- Shooter\n- Aligner\n- T ongue\n- Descorer\nROBOT 14\n- Research \n- Robot Type\n- Personalize\n- Building\n- Chassis\n-  Intake \n-  Aligner\n- T ongue\n-  Color Sorting \n- Programming\n- Simulator\n- TAI VEX Practice\nROBOT 25\n-Reﬂection \n-CAD\n-Intake Design\n-Building\n- Sprocket\n- Roof\n- Mat Support\n- Descorer\n- Programming\n-TAI VEX HS 26\n6\n- Reﬂection \n- Shooter Design\n- Intaker Design\n7\n16\n43\n91\n227\n293", "Table of Contents\nTeam Management8\nRobot 2.27\n- Wing\n- Game Strategies\n- Skills Strategies Ver. 3\n- Club Website\n- Club Budget\n- Practice\n- T eam Impact\n- Club Organization\n- VEX Materials\n- 3D Printing\n- CNC\n- Acrylic Bending Machine\n-Building\n- Tongue\n- Descorer\n- Aligner\n- Wing\n- Programming\n- Simulator\n-Skills Strategies Ver. 2\n-PAS Signature\n398\n419\n393\n415", "TEAM & PROJECT MANAGEMENT\nCOMPETITION ANALYSIS\nTEAM                                                    \nColor Coding \nDESIGN\n#1\nJan - Feb\nIDENTIFY \nTHE \nPROBLEM\n#2\nBRAINSTORM \nSOLUTION #3\nTESTING\nSOLUTION\n#4\nSELECT\nSOLUTION\nPROGRAMMING", "Engineering Design \nProcess\n#1\nJan - Feb\nIDENTIFY THE \nPROBLEM\n#2\nBRAINSTORM \nSOLUTION\n#3\nTESTING\nSOLUTION\n#4\nSELECT\nSOLUTION\nIdentify Problem througn Testing\nGame Analysis/Rules\nResearch\nBrainstorm possible solution\nList Pros and Cons\nGraphs and Tables\nExperiment\nAnalysis and Decisions\nCAD, Prototype", "01\nTEAM\n", "Pennlaine\nTeam Members\n- Grade 11\n- Club President & T eam Leader\n- 3rd year in VEX\n- “Somewhere between calculation and \nchaos”\n9", "Adam\nTeam Members\n- Grade 11\n- 2-year VEX experience\n- Programmer/main driver\n- Adaptive, fast learner\n10", "Anthony\nTeam Members\n- Grade 11\n- Complete newbie in Vex standards\n- Builder/CAD/Art Lead\n- “There is no prize to perfection, only an \nend to pursuit.”\n11", "Benjamin\nTeam Members\n- Grade 11\n- First year in VEX\n- Builder/Planning/Programming\n- “Join in the middle of the season......More”\n12", "Jefferson\nTeam Members\n- Grade 11\n- First year in VEX \n- Build, repair , improve\n13", "Cecilia\nTeam Members\n- Grade 11\n- First year in VEX\n- Scout/PR/Notebook taker\n- “Mascot? Peacemaker? Pistachio!”\n14", "Team Management\nRules and Goals for this year:\n1. Always record the process/take pictures when \nﬁnished one part, then put it in group chat and \nnotebook.\n2. Always ask questions on the whole team’s group chat \nand discuss for the optimal solution\n3. Always ask before changing what we planned to do \nby providing reasons and ask for the whole team’s \napproval\na. If teammates have different opinions, we vote. \nb.If it meets a tie, the team leader shall decide, but \nthe whole team takes the responsibility; no one is to \nblame. \n4. GET QUALIFIED INTO WORLDS!!!\nPennlaine 5/29\nTeam Organization & Info\n15", "02\nGAME ANALYSIS", "Project Management\nMeeting Logs\nJefferson 6/9\n2025/06/09 — 45 minutes\nDiscussion topics\nT eams:\n- 3 teams (1 junior high, 2 high school)\n- Each team with 4-6 teammates\n- We decided the both teams’ leader by voting\n- We also discussed and decided everyone’s job and \nposition in the team\n- Stated general rules for the team (See Page 2)\nGame Manual:\n- We watched the animation of the 2025-26 VEX High \nStakes together\n- Everyone was told to ﬁnish reading the game manual \nby next Thursday\n 17\nWho is here Goals for today’s meeting\n- Pennlaine\n- Adam\n- Anthony\n- Jefferson \nDiscuss\n- Teams\n- Game Manual\n", "Identify the problem\nGame rules & Scoring\nPennlaine 6/10\nField View\n18", "Identify the problem\nField Setup and Object\n● 88 Blocks in total, 44 Blocks for each color , both \ncontaining:\n○ 2 Preloads\n○ 12 Match Loads\n○ 18 that start the Match in predetermined locations \non the Field\n○ 12 that start the Match in Loaders\n● 4 Loaders\n● 4 Goals\n○ 2 Long Goals (15 utmost each)\n○ 2 Center Goals, one Upper and one Lower (7 utmost \neach)\n● 2 Park Zones\nPennlaine 6/10\n19", "Identify the problem\nGame Overview\nGame Phases:\n● Autonomous Period (15 seconds): Robots operate \nsolely on pre-programmed instructions.\n● Driver-Controlled Period (1 minute 45 seconds): T eams \ncontrol their robots manually to score points.\nAutonomous Bonus:\n● Awarded to the alliance with the most points at the \nend of the Autonomous Period (+10 points).\nAutonomous Win Point (AWP):\n● Score at least 7 blocks of our color.\n● Score blocks into at least 3 goals out of 4 on the ﬁeld.\n● At least 3 blocks of our color removed from Loaders \nadjacent to the Alliance’s Alliance Station (upmost 6 \nblocks available)\n● Neither Robot is contacting the Park Zone barrier.\n    → THESE WILL BE MODIFIED FOR EVENTS QUALIFYING TO \nWORLDS AFTER 2025/9/4,. We will put it in UPDATES\nPennlaine 6/1020", "Identify the problem\nGame Scoring\nScoring:\n● Blocks Scored: Each block scored into a goal is worth \n3 points.\n● An Alliance Controls a Controlled Zone: \n○ Scored in Long Goal:  10 points\n○ Scored in Upper Center Goal:  8 points\n○ Scored in Lower Center Goal:  6 points\nPennlaine 6/1021", "Identify the problem\nGame Scoring\nScoring: \n● Robots earn points by parking into the parking zone:\n○ 1 robot: 8 points\n○ Both robots: 30 points\n○ A parked robot will only be touching the inside \nﬂoor of the parking zone and/or the parking zone \nobjects and/or another parked robot.\nPennlaine 6/1022", "Identify the problem\nGame Scoring Analysis\nWe would like to develop a strategy that we can \nimplement to guarantee the most points received, as \nwe all know, it is too idealistic to get all the scores. \n1. Double parking is the highest single action\n2. Gaining the control bonus is essential for higher \nscoring, as both the control bonus and scoring \nblocks give points. \n3. The middle goals can be considered a game \nchanger , as a robot only needs to score in one ball \nto control. \n Pennlaine 6/20\n23", "Identify the problem\nGame Speciﬁc Rules\n1. Contacting the Alliance’s Park Zone when the match \nstarts\n2. Size limit \na. 18” x 18” x 18” starting size\nb. extension is restricted to 22” x 22” x 22”\n3. 1 preload\n4. Can’t cross Autonomous Line during Autonomous \nperiod\n5. Drive T eam Members may introduce Match Load \nBlocks by placing them into Loaders adjacent to their \nAlliance Station.\n6. Robots cannot directly affect blocks in enclosed \nsections of Goals (Long Goals)\n7 .Cannot contact the other Alliance’s parking zone in \nthe last 20 seconds\n8. Limited to 12 individual pieces of plastic not larger \nthan 4” x 8” x 0.070”\n9 . 3D printed parts for any purpose are prohibited\nPennlaine 6/20\n24", "Identify the problem\nGame Overview\nEach robot begins a match contacting their parking \nzone and can’t cross the Autonomous line. Robots can \nat most score fully 44  blocks into four goals and have \nboth the alliance’s robots parked. This would result in \nthe highest possible score if this team manages to win \nthe autonomous bonus also. Of course, this is nearly \nimpossible. \nProblem Statement:\n● There are 4 goals. The Drive T eam Members shall \ncollaborate seamlessly with the robot/the driver so it \ncan intake the blocks as efficient as possible. Robots \nshall also defense or keep inputting blocks so that \ntheir scored blocks maintain at the center.\nPennlaine 6/2225", "Identify the problem\nGame Strategies\n1. Parking is important (+30 to different teams would \nmake a 60 point difference)\n2. Will need a lot of cooperation within Drive T eam \nMembers for both introducing Match Loads and \nkeeping an eye on whether the blocks are removed \nor changed positions from the control zones by the \nopposing alliance\n3. Collaboration with our alliance is crucial so that we \ncan achieve a higher score\nDesign Statement:\nDesign, build, and compete with a robot that can \neffectively score into different heights of goals, and \npark (make space for our teammate to park as well). \nPennlaine 6/2226", "Identify the problem\nGame Contraints\n1. Robot must be 18” x 18” x 18”\n2. Expansion limited to 22’’ x 22’’ x 22’’\n3. VEX materials only\n4. Custom plastic material limited to 12 individual \npieces of plastic not larger than 4” x 8” x 0.070”\n5. At most 88W motor power in total\n6. First competition in late September\nPennlaine 6/2227", "Identify the problem\nSkills Field\nThe Skills ﬁeld and rules is different from regular \nmatches. However , rules not mentioned explicitly follow \nregular matches. \nPennlaine 7/1\n28", "Identify the problem Cecilia 7/1\nPoints  Achievements Total \nPoints\n1  Scored one block 44\n5  Fill a Long Goal Control \nZone 10\n10  Fill a Central Goal Control \nZone 20\n5  Clear a Park Zone 10\n5  Clear a Loader 20\n15  Park robot 15\n 119\nSkills Scoring\n29", "Identify the problem\nGame Updates\nVersion 0.2 (June 5, 2025)\n● Deﬁnitions & Clariﬁcations\n○ Lifting/T rapping: Preventing a Robot already off the \nﬂoor from returning may count as Lifting/T rapping.\n○ Control Zone: Boundaries clariﬁed; a Long Goal \nControl Zone holds up to 3 Blocks between (not \nincluding) the white tape lines.\n○ Scored Blocks: Must be in contact with the inside \nsurface of the plastic trough.\n● Autonomous & Game Play\n○ T eams committing any Violation during \nAutonomous make their alliance ineligible for \nAutonomous Win Point; bonus goes to opposing \nalliance.\n○ Blocks that engage over the Autonomous Line \n(including objects/robots) are considered in \ncontact with it.\n○ Drive T eam may add Match Load Blocks to Loaders \nduring Autonomous and Driver Controlled Periods.\nPennlaine 6/1230", "Identify the problem\nGame Updates\n● Robot Size & Expansion\n○ Robots must ﬁt within a 22\" × 22\" × 22\" box; \nvertical/horizontal expansion limits clariﬁed.\n● Interactions with Field & Blocks\n○ Robots cannot contact Blocks fully inside enclosed \nLong Goal sections, but open sections are allowed.\n○ Blocks bouncing out of Loaders during loading are \nnot violations.\n● Robot Rules & Hardware\n○ License plates must remain stationary; VEX IQ pins \nare no longer allowed for mounting.\nPennlaine 6/2231", "Identify the problem\nGame Updates\nVersion 1.0 (June 26, 2025)\n● Autonomous Line & Blocks\n○ <SG7eii> updated: all Blocks on the other side of the \nAutonomous Line are included.\n○ <RSC2a> updated: Blocks contacting a Robot at the \nend of a Robot Skills Match do not count as \nScored.\n○ <RSC3> updated: Robot Skills Matches do not \ninclude Match Load Blocks, and blocks leaving the \nﬁeld are not returned.\n● Robot Size \n○ <R5> revised: Event Partners can use any sizing tool \nto measure robot starting dimensions.\nPennlaine 8/232", "Identify the problem\nGame Updates\nVersion 1.1 (August 7 , 2025)\n● Major Violations / Block Removal\n○ <SG4> updated: Strategic or intentional removal of \n3 or more Blocks from the Field in a single Match \nnow results in a Major Violation.\n● Autonomous & Incidental Violations\n○ <SG7e> & <SG8b> updated: Incidental Violations of \n<SG7> will not be penalized and do not \nautomatically give Autonomous Bonus to the \nopponent.\n● Loader Rules & Drive T eam Actions\n○ <SG9> updated: Drive T eam Members may use both \nhands when introducing Blocks into a Loader.\n○ <SG9d> clariﬁed: Blocks bouncing out of the bottom \nopening of a Loader after proper introduction are \nnot violations.\n○ <SG9> new bullet: Blocks can only be added to \nLoaders containing 5 or fewer Blocks.\nPennlaine 8/1033", "Identify the problem\nGame Updates\nVersion 2.0 (September 4, 2025)\n● Field & Zone Deﬁnitions\n○ Loader: Deﬁnition clariﬁed.\n○ Park Zone: Includes ﬂoor tiles bordered by ﬁeld \nperimeter and plastic extrusions; dimensions 18.87” \n× 16.86”. Robots may be parked here at the end of a \nMatch.\n○ SC2a: Clariﬁed inside surfaces for scoring. Figure \nSC2-2 updated.\n● Autonomous Win Point (Worlds-qualifying events)\n○ Alliance must complete all of the following:\n○ ≥10 Blocks of alliance color scored.\n○ ≥3 Goals with at least 2 scored Blocks of alliance \ncolor\n○ ≥3 Blocks removed from Loaders adjacent to \nAlliance Station.\n○ Neither Robot is contacting the Park Zone barrier.\nPennlaine 9/534", "Identify the problem\nGame Updates\n● SG / Loader Updates\n○ <SG4>: “Intentionally/strategically” removed to \nsimplify refereeing.\n○ <SG9>: Loader rules clariﬁed:\n○ Only add Blocks if no blocks partially/fully in \norange portion.\n○ Illegal Match Loads considered 2 points for Match \nAffecting calculations.\n○ Escalation to Major Violation for repeated Minor \nViolations (6+ illegal Match Loads in a single \nQualiﬁcation Match, or 3+ Qualiﬁcation Matches \nwith ≥1 illegal Match Load).\n○ <SG11>: Protect Robots partially in their own Park \nZone; no contact with opposing Park Zone or \nopponent Robots in last 20 seconds.\nPennlaine 9/535", "Identify the problem\nGame Updates\nVersion 2.1 (October 9, 2025)\n● Scoring & Match Result Determination\n○ <SC1> — Match scores must NOT be changed due \nto Violations during the Match.\n● Conduct, Behavior , & Event Protocol\n○ <GG17> — Holding\n■ A Holding count begins immediately\n■ Once the Head Referee observes a suspected \nHolding interaction\nPennlaine 10/1436", "Identify the problem\nGame Updates\n● Gameplay & Scoring Object Handling\n○ <SG4a>\n■ Added to clarify handling of blocks that should \nhave exited the Field but were deﬂected back into \nplay\n○ <SG9> — Match Loads\n■ Match Loads must be introduced one at a time\n■ Added a Violation note reinforcing this \nrequirement.\n○ <SG10> — Reaching vs Anchoring\n■ Added a new paragraph clarifying:\n■ Robots may reach into open portions of a Goal \nonly while actively moving Blocks\n■ If a Robot:\n● Reaches into a Goal\n● Remains there without actively moving Blocks\n■ The Head Referee should verbally warn the T eam \nand issue a Violation if the Robot remains\nPennlaine37 10/14", "Identify the problem\nGame Updates\n● Added Offensive, Defensive, and Goalkeeping as new \nterms.\n○ Offensive: A Robot is Offensive while it is engaged \nin actions that could directly increase its Alliance's \nscore for the current Match.\n○ Defensive: A Robot is Defensive while it is engaged \nin actions that cannot increase its Alliance's score \nfor the current Match, and instead limits an \nopponent’s ability to score or play the game.\n○ A robot is Goalkeeping while it is reaching into any \nopen portion of a Goal while not attempting to \nchange the score of the Match through legal \nmeans.\nPennlaine 12/438", "03\nBRAINSTORMING \n& DECISIONS", "Brainstorming \nDeﬁning the Robot\nCHASSIS\n1. Structural requirements/limits\n2. Drivetrain Type\n3. Gear Ratio\n4. Wheels\n5. Motors/Watts\nSUPER STRUCTURE\n1. Intake System\n2. Shooter System\n3. Aligner\n4. T ongue\n5. Descorer\nPennlaine40 7/8", "Project Management Jefferson 7/8\n2024/07/08 — 65 minutes\nBefore starting to build our chassis, we drafted a to do \nlist so we can follow along\nT o Do List:\n1. Evaluate different drivetrain types \n2. Evaluate chassis speed\n3. Compare different motors \n4. Experiment different types of wheels \n5. Choose the best ﬁt above structures\n6. Start off doing CAD for the chassis. \nBuilding will start at mid-late July.\nMeeting Logs\n41\nWho is here Goals for today’s meeting\n- Pennlaine\n- Adam\n- Anthony\n- Jefferson\n- Brainstorm Planning\n", "Project Management Jefferson 7/8\nBrainstorm Timeline\n42\nTasks 7/8-7/14 7/15-7/21\nChassis \n \nIntake \nShooter \nDescorer \nAligner \nTongue \nT asks per member:\n- Chassis: Adam, Pennlaine\n- Intake: Jefferson, Anthony\n- Shooter: Pennlaine, Adam\n- Descorer: Pennlaine, Adam\n- Aligner: Anthony\n- T ongue: Jefferson", "Identify the problem\nChassis Requirements\nParking Requirements\nCenter of Gravity: at either \nfront or back, for the car’s \none side to leave the ground.\nWheels: Can’t use a lot of \nwheels or else it might block \nthe parking process\nSpeed: can’t be too fast or \nelse torque would be too \nsmall.\nLateral Stiffness: the bar \nstructure across should be \nplace at a higher position\nCecilia 7/843\nAccording to the game manual, the chassis for this \nseason should achieve these requirements:", "Brainstorming \nChassis Type\nParking Brainstorming\nPennlaine44\nParking Boards\nThis year’s Parking Zone bars resembles 2022-23 Spin \nUp obstacle.  \nT eams attached customized plastic boards on the \nchassis to avoid robots’ gears getting stuck with the \nbars. . \n7/8", "Brainstorming \nChassis Type\nDrivetrain Brainstorming Pros & Cons \nPennlaine\nDrivetrain \nType Pros Cons\nT ank Drive\nSimple, robust, easy to \nbuild and program, strong \npushing power , good for \nstraight-line movement.\nLimited mobility; can only \nmove forward and \nbackward or turn; less \nmaneuverable than other \ndrives.\nH Drive\nOmnidirectional movement \nwith minimal complexity, \nsimple implementation of \nlateral movement, good for \nmaintaining stability.\nLess power and pushing \nforce compared to tank \ndrive, less precise than X \ndrive, can be inefficient if \nnot well implemented.\nMecanum \nDrive\nOmnidirectional movement \nwith high control and \nbetter traction than typical \nomnidirectional drives is \ngood for smooth \nmovement over obstacles.\nEither too fast or too slow \n(600 rpm or 200 rpm) b/c \none motor controls one \nwheel. Also, makes the \nchassis relatively big.\nX Drive\nHighly maneuverable, \nquick omnidirectional \nmovement, good for agile \nrobots that need precise, \nquick adjustments.\nMore complex \nmechanically, it can be \nharder to control and have \nless traction and power for \npushing compared to tank \ndrive.\n45\nMeccanum Drive H Drive X Drive\n7/8", "Selecting Solution\nＷe decided to evaluate chassis performances based on 6 \nmetrics, with varied proportions. \n1. Maneuverability: weights highest (30%) due to frequent \nalignment when scoring. This aspect reﬂects its agility, \nresponsiveness, and omni-directional ability.\n2. Stability: weights 25% was prioritized to resist defense. \nThis relates to how robust it is, its unlikeliness to break. \n3. Speed: weights 10%, it determines the robots ability to \nperform actions across the ﬁeld fast enough. \n4. Ease of Build: also weights 10%, some chassis are \ninherently rather highly difficult to build, or taking up too \nmuch time, we might not be able to make maximize its \nperformance.\n5. Maintenance: rather important(15%), this reﬂects the \ndifficulty of ﬁxing or rebuilding the chassis, which would \naffect our schedules mainly during mid-season\n6. Versatility: also weighting 10%, this is affected by the \neasiness to work with different speed, torque, sizes of the \ndrivetrain, so that we can change our chassis to different \nsetups throughout the season. \nChassis Type\nPennlaine46 7/8", "Selecting Solution\nThe resulting scoring for each chassis is shown:\nFrom the scores we gave to each drive after evaluation, \nwe decided to work on tank drives for our robot. \nChassis Type\nPennlaine\n47 7/8", "Selecting Solution\nREASONS FOR REJECTION\nWe rejected the other 3 chassis because:\nChassis Type\nPennlaine48\nType Reason for Rejection\nX Drive Insufficient pushing power , high \ndifficulty\nMecanum Large size limited parking\nH Drive Low lateral stability & accuracy\n7/8", "Testing Solution\nChassis Speed\nWe made a 36T:60T (360rpm), 60T:84T (430 rpm), and \n36T:48T (450 rpm) chassis during off-season:\nPennlaine 7/1049\n360 RPM 430 RPM 450 RPM", "Selecting Solution\nChassis Speed\nWe are analyzing which chassis speed gives the best \nperformance overall, based on its speed, driver \ncontrol, and torque. \nWe decided a 450 rpm chassis would be optimal after \nevaluation for mainly (1)easier driver control, and \n(2)stronger torque, as demonstrated in the below \ngraph.\nPennlaine50\nConfigurat\nion\nSpeed \n(RPM)\nDriver \nControl\nSpeed\nTorque\nOverall \nEvaluatio\nn\n36T : 60T 360 Too slow High \n(0.58Nm)\nToo slow \nfor \ncompetitiv\ne play\n60T : 84T 430 Good Medium \n(0.48Nm)\nBalance \nbut \nrestrictive\n36T : 48T 450 Excellent Medium \n(0.47Nm)\nBest \nOverall\n7/10", "Brainstorming \nChassis Cartridge\nT o select the motor cartridges, which determines the \nspeed and torque of the chassis, we made comparisons \nof each cartridges. \n100 RPM Red Cartridges:\n- Strong/Slow\n- Usually used for mechanisms requiring extra strength\n200 RPM Green Cartridges:\n- Maintains the green cartridge preset \n- Usually used for mechanisms requiring a balance of \nstrength and speed\n600 RPM Blue Cartridges:\n- Fast/ Low Friction\n- Usually used for when requiring extra speed\nSource (VEX official):\nVEX motors: https://kb.vex.com/hc/en-us/articles/360044325872-Understanding-V5-Smart-Motor-11W-Performance, \nPennlaine51\n 7/10", "Selecting Solution\nChassis Gear Ratio\nSince torque and speed are inversely portional ↓, we \ncan choose any gear ratio to cater to the motors to \nachieve the desired chassis speed, 450 RPM. \nBecause 450 RPM is relatively close to 600 rpm, we \ndecided to use blue cartridges:\n1. Smaller gear teeth differences (would be 4:3)\n2. less volume interference to superstructure \n Pennlaine52\nCartridge\nRaw \nSpeed \n(RPM)\nGear \nRatio\nPossible T eeth \nCombo\nStructural \nImpact\nOverall \nEvaluatio\nn\nRed 100 2:9\n✖ (Not possible \nwith VEX gears, \nnearest=300/600)\n✖ ✖\nGreen 200 4:9\n36:72T is the \nnearest ratio → \ngives 400rpm\nLarge gears \nlimit \ninternal \nstructure\nBalanced \nbut \nrestrictive\nBlue 600 4:3 48:36T\nCompact, \neasy to \nintegrate\nBest \nOverall\n7/10", "Testing Solution\nChassis Analysis\nPower & Efficiency Analysis:\nChoosing 600 rpm cartidriges for our chassis, we can \nanalyze the efficiency of our chassis. \nWe geared the chassis to 450 rpm, which is 75% of the \n600 rpm full speed, just in between the range which \ngives the largest power of the motor. \nSource (VEX official):\nT orque-speed curve: \nhttps://kb.vex.com/hc/en-us/articles/360044325872-Understanding-V5-Smart-Motor-11W-Performance, \nPennlaine\n53\n←\nBased on the motor \ncurve, increasing speed \nto about 55% - 75% of \nthe motor’s max RPM \nsigniﬁcantly increases \nthe power to above 12 \nWatts.\n7/10", "Selecting Solution\nChassis Motors\nBecause we decided to use 600rpm blue cartridges, 6 \n11-Watt-motors would be optimal to contribute because \nwe agree that a higher strength is required, when we \nhave less torque. \nWith the available 22 Watts left, there could be 3 \ncombinations (for using up to the max Watts):\nSource (VEX official):\nVEX motors: https://kb.vex.com/hc/en-us/articles/360044325872-Understanding-V5-Smart-Motor-11W-Performance, \nJefferson\n5.5 Watts Motor 11 Watts Motor\n0 2\n2 1\n4 0\n54 7/10", "Brainstorming \nChassis Wheel Type\nGiven the emphasis on above requirements, robots \nshould be designed for quick, agile movement. \nWheel Comparison:\nSource (VEX official):\nVEX wheels: https://kb.vex.com/hc/en-us/articles/38888566734612-Understanding-VEX-V5-Wheels\nJefferson\nOmni-Wheels\n- more lateral movement\n- less traction\nTraction-Wheels\n- less likely to get pushed\n- less lateral movement\n55 7/11", "Testing Solution\nChassis Wheel Type\nTEST 1\nT o evaluate the maneuverability & rotation efficiency \nof different wheel types.\nT est Procedure:\n1. Run 10 rotations program\n2. Start stopwatch as program starts \n3. Stop stopwatch when prototype stops\n4. Record results (in seconds)\nProgram Snippet:c\nJefferson 7/1156\nvoid test()\n{\n // Run chassis for 10 rotations\n chassis.DriveL.resetPosition();\n chassis.DriveR.resetPosition();\n Controller1.Screen.clearScreen();\n Controller1.Screen.setCursor(1, 1);\n Controller1.Screen.print(\"Start!\");\n wait(0.05, sec);\n chassis.DriveL.spinFor(forward, 10, turns, false);\n chassis.DriveR.spinFor(forward, 10, turns, true);\n Controller1.Screen.setCursor(2, 1);\n Controller1.Screen.print(\"Finish!\");\n}", "Testing Solution\nChassis Wheel Type\nTEST 1\nResults\nAverage Rotation Time (seconds):\nJefferson 7/1157\nT rial 4 Omni Wheels 4 T raction Omni + \nT raction\n1 1.24 1.36 1.30\n2 1.29 1.41 1.34\n3 1.22 1.38 1.28\n4 1.31 1.44 1.36\n5 1.27 1.35 1.33\n6 1.26 1.47 1.32\n7 1.33 1.42 1.37\n8 1.21 1.39 1.29\n9 1.28 1.45 1.35\n10 1.25 1.40 1.31\n4 Omni 4 Traction Omni + Traction\n1.27 1.41 1.32", "Testing Solution\nChassis Wheel Type\nTEST 1\nBelow plot shows TEST 1 results:\nJefferson 7/1158\n", "Selecting Solution\nChassis Wheel Type\nTEST 1\nAnalysis\nThe 4 omni-wheel conﬁguration achieved the lowest \naverage rotation time and the smallest variation, \nindicating turning efficiency. \nTraction wheels required additional time due to \nincreased friction, which limits rotational speed. \nThe mixed conﬁguration improved turning compared \nto full traction wheels but remained slower than full \nomni wheels.\nDecision\nThis year’s game objects and goals, and actions are \nrelatively few, therefore, fast escape and scoring would \nbe essential. Therefore, the omni-wheel type was \nselected for the ﬁnal drivetrain.\nJefferson59 7/11", "Testing Solution\nChassis Wheel Type\nTEST 2\nT o evaluate how efficiently the robot aligns with a \ntarget wall using turning and forward motion (test \nLATERAL)\nT est Procedure:\n1. Place robot 1.5 m from target at 30° offset\n2. Start robot facing away from target\n3. Use joystick to align and drive to target\n4. Stop timer when robot is fully aligned\nRecord results\nJefferson60\nTrial 4 Omni Wheels 4 Traction \nWheels\nOmni + Traction\n1 2.74 3.58 3.02\n2 2.68 3.44 2.96\n3 2.81 3.62 3.15\n4 2.72 3.51 3.08\n5 2.77 3.47 2.99\n6 2.69 3.55 3.11\n7 2.83 3.60 3.04\n8 2.71 3.42 2.92\n7/11", "Testing Solution\nChassis Wheel Type\nTEST 2\nAverage Rotation Time (seconds):\nBelow plot shows TEST 2 results:\nJefferson61\n4 Omni 4 Traction Omni + Traction\n2.74 3.52 3.03\n7/11", "Selecting Solution\nChassis Wheel Type\nTEST 2\nAnalysis\nThe omni-wheel conﬁguration enables faster turning \nand smoother arc motion, reducing the time required \nfor target alignment. \nTraction wheels required additional corrective \nmovements due to higher resistance, increasing \nalignment time.\nDecision\nAlignment is essential this year , since there are 4 goals \nand 8 open sections for scoring in total, each requiring \nrelatively high precision when scoring, therefore, fast \nand accurate lateral adjustments are essential. \nJefferson62 7/11", "Brainstorming \nChassis Wheel Size\nT o select the motor cartridges, which determines the \nspeed and torque of the chassis, we made \ncomparisons of each cartridges. \n2.75’’ Omni Wheel:\n- Great center of gravity\n- Slow\n3.25’’ Omni Wheel:\n- Balance in speed and strength\n4’’ Omni Wheel:\n- High mobility/speed\nSource (VEX official):\nVEX wheels: https://kb.vex.com/hc/en-us/articles/38888566734612-Understanding-VEX-V5-Wheels\nPennlaine63\n 7/12", "Selecting Solution\nChassis Wheel Size\nWe evaluated 4 aspects that would affect our choice of \nwheel size:\nSpeed: 25%, it plays a relatively important aspect for \nthe chassis, especially for this season. \nCenter of Gravity: 20%, it affects the defensibility and \naccuracy of the chassis. \nAcceleration: 25%, it determines the efficiency of the \nchassis. \nStrength: 30%, it is our prioritization since our chassis \nhas a relatively high speed\nPennlaine64 7/12", "Selecting Solution\nChassis Wheel Size\nThe resulting scoring for each chassis is plotted:\nFinal Decision:\nThe 3.25” omni-wheel achieved the best balance of \ncomprehensively.\nPennlaine65\n 7/12", "Selecting Solution\nChassis Summary\nThe ultimate drivetrain setup is summarized in the \nbelow table:\nThe trade-offs of our chassis setup is also summarized:\nWe have emphasized mobility for this season, therefore \nour chassis setup is tailored to this strategy. \nJefferson66\nComponent Selection Impact\nGear Ratio 36T:48T (450 RPM) Compact gearbox\nWheels 3.25” Omni Low COG, high \nagility\nMotors 6×11W Blue High torque\nType T ank High Compatibility \n& Maneuverability\nT rade-off Beneﬁt Limitation\nHigh speed Agile movement Less strength\nOmni \nwheels Maneuverability Reduced traction\n7/12", "Selecting Solution\nChassis CAD\nThe ultimate drivetrain setup is illustrated with the CAD \nprovided. \nJefferson\n67 7/14", "Project Management Jefferson 7/12\n2024/07/14 — 30 minutes\nAfter developing an outline of our chassis and its CAD, \nwe established another to do list in today’s meeting:\n1. Evaluate and analyze different intake design\n- Research and compare\n2. Brainstorm shooter design and structure\n3. Analyze the function of the aligner , tongue, and \ndescorer; brainstorm design\nMeeting Logs\n68\nWho is here Goals for today’s meeting\n- Pennlaine\n- Adam\n- Anthony\n- Jefferson\n- Brainstorm Planning\n", "Identify the problem\nIntake\nGame Object Analysis\nThis year’s game object is a ball like block, \nbut with 16 faces, making each rotating \nmoment having different sizes. \nField Object Analysis\nThe robot has to be able to load blocks from the \nloader , therefore a independent loader mech is \nrequired to help guide the blocks into the intake \nsystem. \nRequirements\nThe intake system needs adequate amount of \ncompression to smoothly tackle the inconsistent \nintaked size. \nPennlaine 7/1569\n", "Brainstorming \nIntake\nPennlaine70\nHorizontal Flex Wheels:\nThis year’s game object is similar to 2020-21 Change Up \ngame season. \nPush Back’s loader resembles Change Up’s goal, and \nthe meta that year is the horizontal ﬂex wheels, which \nallows direct intaking from the vertical tubes.\n7/15", "Brainstorming \nIntake\nPennlaine71\nFlex Wheels in a row:\nLast season (2024-25 High Stakes) had the intake meta \nof ﬂex wheels, this mech is (ideally) applicable for this \nseason’s game objects as well. \n7/15", "Brainstorming \nIntake\nPennlaine72\nHorizontal Flex Wheels:\nThis year’s game object is also similar to the 2018-19 \nT urning Point game season. \nBoth seasons need to intake ball-like objects from the \nground, and the meta that year is the sprockets with \nrubberbands and/or with anti-static mats. \n7/15", "Brainstorming \nIntake\nIntake \nType Pros Cons\nFlex \nwheels \n(horizontal\n)\nMany teams use it as a \nclaw structure which is \nﬂexible and can control \nwhen (possibly optical) to \nintake it\nMeans that it would require \nat least two motors, 1 for 1 \nside to intake. Also, the \ndesign would make the \nrobot longer since the whole \nclaw is near the ground.\nPennlaine\n73\nEarly Season prototypes from Youtube\n7/15", "Brainstorming \nIntake\nIntake Brainstorming PROS & CONS\nIntake \nType Pros Cons\nFlex \nwheels\n(in a row)\nPretty simple and \nstraightforward, especially \nthat we experienced with \nthis type of intake last year.\nMight provide less elasticity \nfor space, height and the \ngame object. \nPennlaine\n74\nEarly Season prototypes from Youtube\n7/15", "Brainstorming \nIntake\nIntake Brainstorming PROS & CONS\nIntake \nType Pros Cons\nSprockets \nwith \nRubber \nBands\nAlso quite simple and \nstraightforward. We \nexperienced with this \nstructure in the over under \nseason. It is also very \nﬂexible and can grab the \nballs pretty well with its \nhigh friction. \nRubber bands might snap \nduring the game and would \nhurt the intake efficiency a \nlot.\nPennlaine\n75\nEarly Season prototypes from Youtube\n7/15", "Selecting Solution\nIntake\nWe decided that we evaluate the intake types together , \nconsidering that it helps to objectively evaluate and \ndecide. A decent intake system should demonstrate \nhigh scores in:\n1. Reliability: how stable and consistent it is\n2. Efficiency: the speed and smoothness\n3. Ease of build: how fast/difficult we can build it\n4. Volume taken: how much it uptakes of the robot size\n5. Motor Usage: motors/voltages does it require\n6. Versatility: how easy it is to change/modify the \ndesign\nAnthony\n76 7/15", "Selecting Solution\nIntake\nAfter team discussion and brainstorming, we have \ndecided our robot’s intake setup:\n- The control zone of the long goal would be an \nimportant part, so a robust intake system would \ndominate the ﬁeld. \n- The lower middle goal can use the intake system to \nscore  by reversing. \n- We decided to go for an intake that is sprockets with \nrubber bands, because the compression amount is \nlarger , allowing less accurate heights and structure, \nmore compatibility. \nFor Future Building:\n1. Decide whether we want it to intake and score on the \nsame side or opposite side\n2. Decide if we want to do color sorting or store other \nteams’ color’s balls. \nAnthony77 7/15", "Identify the problem\nShooter\nGame Object Analysis\nSImilar to the intake, the shooter system shall have an \nadequate amount of compression to \nscore the 16 faces blocks into the goals. \nField Object Analysis\nThe goals have 3 levels: lower middle, upper middle, \nand ong goal. \nAs mentioned before, the intake can score into the \nlower middle goals by reversing itself. \nThis leaves 2 levels of goals that are required to score \ninto. \nRequirements\nThe shooter system shall contain 2 levels of outake \nscoring. \nPennlaine78\n 7/16", "Brainstorming \nShooter\nThe shooter would be connected to the intake, through \nbelts, sprockets, or ﬂex wheels that chain the blocks up \ntowards the goal heights, these are possible solutions:\n1. Pistons\nPistons can alter the height of the outake \nstructure, providing exactly 2 levels of height. \n2. Parallel Structure\nThis is also a commonly seen mech in VEX, using \nmotors to level up and down a structure\n →\n3. Two pathways\nAnother feasible way is to create 2 pathways that \ncan outake to two levels. The path can be \ncontrolled by rotating direction (by motors) or \nblocking mechs (pistons). ↑\nPennlaine79\n 7/16", "Identify the problem\nAligner\nAn aligner is an often overlooked design, but also \nessential because it allows faster and steadier \nscoring/loading. \n1. Loader\nThe aligner for the loader could be tailored to ﬁt \nthe semi-circle shape of the loader tube. \n2. Goals\nThe long goals have a triangular shape under the \nscoring section. The robot can design a triangular \nshape mech. \nHowever , the middle goals’ triangle section are \nrather far from the scoring section, therefore an \naligner that ﬁts the the scoring section might work \nto center the robot. \nPennlaine80\n 7/16", "Brainstorming \nAligner\nPrevious seasons also demonstrated that aligner \nmechs do not essentially need to be independent \nstructures. \n2024-25 High Stakes\nT eams used plastic boards to design aligners that \ncould center them to the wall stakes last year , \nincorporating their plastic boards with the intake \ndesign. \nPennlaine81\nx\n7/16", "Identify the problem\nTongue\nRobots have to load blocks from the loader throughout \nthe game, both from the preset ﬁeld and human player \nloading. \n1. Enter Angle\nThe tongue has to enter the goal with a downward \nangle.\n2. Intake Angle\nThe tongue will then guide the blocks out one by \none with a upward guiding angle into the angle \nsystem\nPennlaine82\n⓵②\n7/16", "Identify the problem\nDescorer\nAs we have ﬁnished brainstorming the offensive \nsuperstructures, we shall think of defensive mechs, we \nwould call it a descorer. \n<SG10> Don’t reach inside enclosed sections of Goals. \nWe would like to design a descorer that would be a \nboard that pushes scored blocks with great force, \neffectively displacing/relocating blocks. \nPennlaine83\n 7/16", "Project Management Jefferson 7/17\n2025/07/17 — 30 minutes\nIn today’s meeting, we discuss the challenges we might \nface in this season’s matches and possible solutions \nwe should import to our design and strategies:\nChallenge 1: \nLoaders might be the fastest way to intake, comparing \nto intaking from the ﬁeld. Without another force \ncoming from the other side stopping it, the intake \nsystem might only push it further away. \nSolution:\nMake the system higher , lighter , more ﬂexible, perhaps \nuse rubber bands. \nMeeting Challenges\n84\nWho is here Goals for today’s meeting\n- Pennlaine\n- Adam\n- Anthony\n- Challenges Analysis\n- Strategy\n", "Project Management\nMeeting Logs\nAnthony\n go for the loaders near the alliance stations\nintake then spit out \nthe opponent blocks \nto the ground\nIntake alliance \ncolor blocks \nthen score\nPros\nIt would be hard for \nopponent to intake \ntheir blocks\nEarly Season Strategy Brainstorming\n85 7/17", "Project Management\nMeeting Logs\nChallenge 2: \nWhen the opponent alliance has already several blocks \non both sides of the long goal, scoring more of our \nblocks might result in them controlling the zone (even 3 \nblocks*3 pts<10 pts!).\nSolution:\nScore in at least 4 more to make this a beneﬁcial \naction.\n- If we don’t have a tally of 4 blocks on our car or in the \nimmediate vicinity \n→ go for another goal or play defense\n- If they are already occupying the control zone\n→ score to push them out and gain points.\nAnthony86 7/17", "04\nROBOT 1", "Project Management\nMeeting  BUILDING PLAN\n1. We should CAD 70% of the robot before starting to build \none, build the CADed part as CAD continues. \n2. We consider being able to go under the long goal a \nneccessary characteristic, our team might stick to this \nidea the whole season after we watched some matches \nand anaylzed\n3. Storage space is important because it seems like most \ndefense happen at the goals and if we need to score \nfrequently with only 3 or 4 balls each time we risk bigger. \n4. We value agility a lot, so we hope we should design a \nchassis that would fulﬁll this goal\nPennlaine 7/2588\nWho is here Goals for today’s meeting\n- Pennlaine\n- Adam\n- Jefferson\n- Establish a pre-building plan\n2025/07/25 — 30 minutes", "Project Management Jefferson 7/8\nPre-Building Timeline\n89\nTasks 7/25-\n8/1\n8/2-\n8/8\n8/9-\n8/15\n8/16-\n8/22\n8/23-\n8/29\nResearch \n \nChassis CAD \nPersonalize \nRobot Types \nPrototype & CAD \nT asks per member:\n- Research: Pennlaine\n- Chassis CAD: Pennlaine, Anthony\n- Personalize: Jefferson\n- Robot Types: Pennlaine, Adam, Anthony\n- Prototype: Pennlaine, Anthony", "Brainstorming \n1. T o fulﬁll our building plan, we cannot build the \nrobots with big trunks because we need to go \nunder the long goal.\nSnail like or C-shaped designs with pistons for a \nhigher level would be a more suitable choice\n2. We decided to make the chassis narrower for more \nagile movements, namely\na. easier parking\nb. better defending\nc. faster turning for scoring around the loaders \nand goals\n3. We prioritize sprockets with rubber bands for \nintaking blocks because of its high compression \nratio, when necessary, we might tie mats around it \nto make the friction higher and to decrease the \ncompression value. \nPennlaine 7/25\nRobot Type\n90", "Brainstorming \nSnacky Cakes\nHowever , it still doesn’t meet our requirement of able \nto go under the long gaol .\nPennlaine\n 7/25\nResearch\nSnacky Cakes\nThis is a newly revealed early \nseason robot that inspired us. \nPrevious robots for this season \nwere of big trunks for storage, \nbut that sacriﬁced the robot’s \nmobility. \nThe robot uses \n- Sprockets to divide the levels\n- control its rotation direction to \noutake the blocks to different level \ngoals.\nThis robot has a\n-  S like design\n-  large storage\n \n91", "Brainstorming \nT akeaways: \nWe can adopt the S-like design and other structures to \nenlarge the storage. T o allow our robot to go under the \nlong goal, we can add pistons at a structure to allow it \nto lower down for the middle goal level.\nPennlaine\n 7/25\nResearch\nSnacky Cakes\nCool thing to note: their parking \nis dope! They use a block to lift \ntheir robot up so that they don’t \ntouch the ﬂoor out of the \nparking zone. \n92", "Identify the problem\nWe are experiencing with different methods of assembling \nthe drivetrain. \nWe want to choose the smoothest assembly method, \nincluding inserts and spacers. \nWe are ﬁrst determining the shaft inserts that would be \ninserted into wheels. We are overwhelmed, not knowing \nwhich insert we should choose, since there are 3 types of \ninserts in total, including old versions that are discontined \nbut we still have lots of stock of. \nPennlaine\nChassis Assembling\n93\nHigh Strength Shaft Insert\nSKU: 276-3881\nGreen Discontinued!\nHigh Strength Shaft Insert\nSKU: 276-3881\nNew Version = Grey\nHigh Strength Shaft Adapter\nSKU: 276-8034\n7/28", "Testing Solution\nWheels Inserts Experiment:\nTEST 1\nT o evaluate the rotational smoothness and friction \ncharacteristics of different shaft inserts to minimize \ndrivetrain effeciency loss.\nT est Procedure\n1. Mount wheel with selected shaft adapter\n2. Run motor at 50% power for 3 seconds\n3. Cut power instantly\n4. Start stopwatch when wheel fully stops\nResults\nPennlaine\nWheels\n94\nT rial Green Insert Grey Insert Gold Adapter\n1 4.21 5.63 7 .44\n2 4.35 5.58 7 .31\n3 4.18 5.71 7 .52\n4 4.27 5.66 7 .39\n5 4.12 5.60 7 .47\n6 4.30 5.69 7 .58\n7 4.25 5.55 7 .36\n8 4.19 5.62 7 .41\n7/28", "Testing Solution\nWheels Inserts Experiment:\nTEST 1\nAverage Spin Time\nBelow plot shows TEST 1 results:\nPennlaine\nWheels\n95\nAdapter Avg Time (s) Relative Friction\nGreen 4.23 High\nGrey 5.63 Medium\nGold 7 .44 Low\n7/28", "Selecting Solution\nWheels Inserts Experiment:\nTEST 1\nAnalysis\nThe gold shaft adapters consistently exhibited the \nlongest free-spin time, indicating the lowest internal \nfriction. \nThe grey insert kit showed moderate performance, \nwhile the green inserts produced the highest \nresistance, resulting in faster deceleration.\nDecision\nThe gold shaft adapters improved efficiency and \nreliability, leading to their selection for both wheels \nand gears.\nPennlaine\nWheels\n96\n 7/28", "Selecting Solution\nChassis Wheels and Gears Spacing\nThis is an exploded view of our wheel assembly set up, \nusing golden shaft adapters. \nr\nPennlaine\n(Golden) High Strength Shaft Adapter\nWheels and Gears\n97 7/28", "Identify the problem\nOn Discord servers, we saw that a lot of teams had \ntheir gears placed on the outer side of the chassis, \ninstead of what we previously experienced with. \nIf the gears were put on the outer side, the wheels \nwould be on the inner side. The drivetrain would be \nmore agile since for every same-degree turn, it results \nin a shorter wheel traveling distance, therefore faster.  \n Pennlaine\nGears Placement\n98\nV .S\nFrom team 99999V on Discord\nGears on the outside\nFrom our off-season training\nGears on the inside\n7/30", "Testing Solution\nGears Placement:\nDeriving and comparing turning speed and efficiency \nof the two chassis.\nThis shows that the ratio of the time the chassis takes \nto make a same angle turn is directly proportional to \nthe ratio of the width between the center and the \nwheels. c\nPennlaine\nGears Placement\n99\n 7/30", "Selecting Solution\nGears Placement:\nFrom the calculation, we decided to build a chassis \nwith the wheels on the inner side and the gears on the \nouter side. \nPennlaine\nGears Placement\n100 7/30", "Selecting Solution\nWe are setting up our gears spacing. \nPennlaine\nGears Spacing\n101 7/30", "Selecting Solution\nWe ﬁnished the CAD of our chassis with new added \ndetails. And we are starting to build the super \nstructure after having brainstormed it.\nPennlaine\nChassis CAD\n102 8/5", "Selecting Solution\n Anthony\nChassis\n103\nChassis Isometric\n8/5", "Selecting Solution\n Anthony\nChassis\n104\nStacked \nmotor for \nbetter intake \nslope for a \nc-bot\n8/5", "Building Solution\nAfter ﬁnishing CADing the chassis and ensured the \nspacing were correct. Our team started off building the \ndrivetrain physically. \nHowever , we decided to leave longer channels since we \nmight need them later. \nPennlaine\nChassis\n105 8/7", "Building Solution Anthony\nChassis\n106 8/7\nSide of chassis explode\n", "Design\nPersonalizing Our Robot\nWe learned from other teams that they dye their gears so that they \nlook nicer. They also anodize their structure parts. So we thought \nthat we would try this this year. We decided we want our robot \nblack and gold, since it’s our team color. \nTherefore we sent 20 2*1*35 and 10 2*1*35 aluminum channels, 10 \n1*1*35 aluminum angles to a factory for anodizing. It costed us \n$3000NTD in total. \nNext up we started to dye gears. We saw this video on Youtube \nfrom team 60470S teaching how to dye vex plastic parts. We \nbought the same type of dye – “Rit Dye” that was instructed. And we \ndecided to make all the plastic parts \nblack. \nDyeing VEX Parts T utorial\nJefferson\nPersonalize\n107 8/10", "Design\nThe video instructed to prepare:\n1. Acetone\n2. Rit Dyemore synthetic ﬁber dye\n3. Stainless steel bowl\n4. Thermometer\n5. Electric stove\nThen it instructs us how to dye:\n1. Water in the bowl until it covers all the VEX parts\n2. Dye + Acetone 1:1 in the bowl \na. looks like they added about ⅔ of one bottle Rit Dye.\n3. Heat it to 190 degrees Farenheits and keep it at that \ntemperature\n4.  Put in the parts\n5. Can add more dye after a few minutes if it doesn’t \nshow color\nJefferson\nPersonalize\n108\n 8/10", "Design\nAside from looking at the Youtube tutorial, we also \nasked our Chemistry teacher. He suggested that the \nacetone is only used to clear the dust or wax on the \nplastic parts, so he actually advised us to just wipe the \nparts slightly with acetone and not boil them with it. He \nmentioned that boiling it for too much time might \ncorrode the plastic and cause it to change shape. \nOur result was successful for the ﬁrst try!\nJefferson\nPersonalize\n109 8/10", "Design\nThis is our process:\n1. Wipe the plastic parts with acetone with a mop rag \n(don’t contact skin or it might damage it!!)\n2. pour a bottle of Rit Dye (BLACK or NAVY) into the bowl \nand start boiling it.\n3. Wait until it boils and softly add the plastic parts.\n4. boil them until all the colors are shown perfectly, \ntakes about 10 minutes.\n5. T ake a look if the dye is enough, dye can be added\nWe are very satisﬁed by the result so we wanted to also \ndye more, including ﬂex wheels. That was a disaster. It \nbecame green. Lesson taught: do not dye ﬂex wheels.\nJefferson\nApparently we did not do \nenough research…\nPersonalize\n110 8/12", "Design\nAnodized and Dyed Parts ﬁnished!\nBy the time we have gotten all \nour new parts for our now \nplanning to build robot, we have \nto develop a solid idea of our \nrobot before we use these parts.\nWe should ﬁrst listed out the most common types of \nbots now, and then listed out their pros and cons. \nOur decisions and planning is listed in more detail in \nthe meeting logs. Now we will start to build our new \nrobot. \nJefferson\nPersonalize\n111 8/13", "Project Management Jefferson 8/11\n2025/08/11 — 80 minutes\nBrainstorming:\nWe compared the most common robot types this \nseason — basket bots, ramp bots, snail bots, and \nparallel linkage bots — and discussed their pros and \ncons. \n(See our comparison on the next pages)\nWe decided that the C-like Design would best ﬁt our \ngoal, as it allows continuous intake and outtake, can \nstay ﬂat enough to go under the long goal, and can \nstore more blocks within the same height.\nMeeting\nWho is here Goals for today’s meeting\n- Pennlaine\n- Adam\n- Anthony\n- compare and decide robot type\n112", "Brainstorming \nThese are most of the types of robots now revealed for \nthis season:\n1. Basket Bots\n- center of gravity would change \nafter every intaked block\n2. Parallel Linkage Bots\n- it has to wait until the platform \nlifts in front of the long goal \nwhere center of gravity becomes high \nand vulnerable to offenses.\nPennlaine\nPROs CONs\n- larger storage \nspace\n- hard to adjust its \nweight management\nPROs CONs\n- short\n- solid storage \nspace\n- slow\n- vulnerable to \noffenses\n8/12\nRobot Type\n113", "Brainstorming \n3. Ramp Bots\n- single ramp makes its storage \nspace relatively small.\n- pistons are carrying the bot’s \nmain mech\n4. S Bots\nPennlaine\nPROs CONs\n- allow two level \nscoring \n- fast\n- light\n- small storage space\n- if no air can’t score \nlong goal\nPROs CONs\n- allow two level \nscoring \n- large storage \nspace\n- heavy\n- relatively few bots \nlike this were \nrevealed\n8/12\nRobot Type\n114", "Brainstorming \n5. C Bots\nPennlaine\nPROs CONs\n- allow two level \nscoring \n- lighter than S \nbots\n- short\n- lighter than S bots\n- defensive\n8/12\nRobot Type\n115", "Selecting Solution Pennlaine 8/12\nCriteria Basket \nBot\nParallel \nLinkage \nBots\nRamp \nBots S Bots C Bots\nCapacity 10 3 3 10 6\nWeight 3 6 10 5 9\nAgibility 3 10 5 5 10\nSpeed 3 5 10 6 9\nEase of \nBuild 9 9 10 6 6\nDefense 5 3 6 9 10\nTOTAL 33 36 44 41 50\nRobot Type\n116\nWe will evaluate these kinds of robots based on 6 aspects:\n1. Capacity: How many blocks it can store\n2. Weight: How heavy the bot is\n3. Agibility: The maneuverability of the bot \n4. Speed: How fast the robot is cable of \n5. Ease of Build: How conﬁdent we are ﬁnishing succesfully\n6. Defense: The bot’s defense ability and performance", "Selecting Solution\nAfter initial researching and evaluation, we preferred C \nbots for several reasons:\n1. It rates the highest of the comprehensive scoring.\n2. it is possible for it to accomplish our goals\n3. relatively few bots are revealed that means we can \ndesign ourself and possibly get an award on our \ndesigns\n4. Now we do’nt see any fatal defects for this kind of \nbots\nPennlaine\nRobot Type \n117 8/13", "Brainstorming \nAfter this decision, we started to \nresearch on C bots.\n1. 7121B BLITZ\na. Thier robot has two levels of \nmotion. \nFirst level uses a belt, and the \nupper one uses ﬂex wheels.\nb.They use plastic boards to \nconnect the curve shape \nneeded for block \nconnection. \nc. They use pistons to adust which \nheight of goals to score in. \nd.They have a well done color sort \nmech\nin the back of the car →\nPennlaine\nResearch\n118\nb c\n8/14", "Brainstorming \n2. 16610A Snacky Cakes\na. Uses the rolling direction of ﬂexwheels\nand sprockets to decide which level\nto outake at\nb. Flex wheel here outtakes to the center \nupper goal.\nc. A piston mech connected with a \ndescore mech that prevents\nthe blocks from falling out \nwhen robot is moving. \nd. Their descore mech looks\nlike one we analyzed before in the ﬁrst\nevent of push back\ne. This bot has relatively less storage \nsince it wastes some anterior to \nposterior space.\nf. Also, it cannot go under the long goal\nPennlaine\nResearch\n119 8/14", "Brainstorming \n3. Robotics is EZ — FLUSHER\na. It has a small truck that can store\nabout 10 blocks\nb. Although they look similar to Snacky \nCakes, they don’t have a real center\ngoal scoring level. They only use the \nlong goal level to drop blocks down. \nc. However , they use that ﬂex wheel to \ndescore middle goals.\nd. They also have descore mech for \nlong goal, which is also combined \nwith the outake mech.\nPennlaine\nResearch\n120\nd\nc\n8/14", "Brainstorming \n4. 334U Supernova Redeem\na. Uses pistons for the shooter part to \nadjust which level to outake at.\nb. Can go under the long goal.\nc. When the piston is retracted, the bot\nhas a sequence of sprockets and\nﬂex wheels that helps with its scoring \nto center goal.\nd. When the piston is extended, the \nmoved ﬂex wheel still acts as the last \nwheel before outtakes. \ne. Well built descore mech that uses a \npassive design without electronic\nmotion. →\nPennlaine\nResearch\n121\na\n8/14", "Selecting Solution\nResearch Conclusion\nCombined with our goals, we now have our own idea of how to \nbuild our bot. \n1. The upper level part should use pistons, like 334U. \n2. T o aim for more storage, we would extend our bot’s anterior to \nposterior storage space, so that it is almost double as only the \nﬁrst level. \n3. Descorer design might not be considered when prototyping \nsince a lot of teams combine descorers with their structure \ndesign, like using their ﬂex wheels at the outake part to descore. \nOur Goal\n1. Prioritize Agility\n2. Storage maximize\nMain Ideas\n1. Low enough to go under the long goal\n2. Fast chassis\n3. As long as possible to store more blocks\n4. Use rubber band sprockets for more ﬂexibility in space\nPennlaine\nResearch\n122 8/14", "Selecting Solution\nRobot Sketch:\nPennlaine\nPrototype\n123 8/18", "Selecting Solution\nThis is when the piston is extended, for long goal \nscoring\nPennlaine\nPrototype\n124 8/18", "Selecting Solution\nExplanation:\nIf the whole bot is shared during two paths for \noutaking, we could save space and make our robot \nlower. \nThe ﬁrst level is slightly ramped so that it could \nconnect to the second level, also because we have \nmotors back there, and the mat (blue) is on it.\nWe will use mats as the ground for ﬁrst level, as its \nshape is determined by the motors and we could easily \nrestructure its shape using left-to-right direction \nstandoffs.\nPennlaine\nPrototype\n125 8/18", "Selecting Solution\nExplanation:\nFor the last sprocket at the ﬁrst level, it connects to the \nsecond level with a plastic board/mat (needs testing, \nbut same idea as 7121B BLITZ) to help the blocks go up. \nThat is why we have to use large diameter sprockets \nwith rubber bands. We have to make sure the rubber \nbands can touch the blocks for all 3 directions. If the \ndiameter is small, the balls might easily get stuck at the \nsprocket’s shaft. A larger sprocket guarantees a larger \namount of compression. \nPennlaine\nPrototype\n126 8/18", "Selecting Solution\nExplanation:\nThe ball then goes to the second level utilizing the ﬁrst \nlevel’s roof as the second level’s ground. The second \nlevel’s roof would be plastic boards or mats. We now \nthink that the second level should be ﬂex wheels, since \nif the ground is already very ﬂexible when the ball is at \nthis point:   it’s roof should not be so compressible or \nelse it would       outake with a weak force. After the ball \nis squeezed        out of the two wheels at the point, it \nenters the               shooter part which is controlled by a \npiston.  \nPennlaine\nPrototype\n127 8/18", "Selecting Solution\nExplanation:\nFor the shooter part, it rotates on an axis and it \ncontains three ﬂex wheels for the outake torque to be \nbigger. The ﬂex wheel which is lower and extending \nfrom the main shooter structure acts as a blockage for \nthe blocks so that it would drop down back to the ﬁrst \nlevel. It rotates at the same direction as the farther \nmost outake ﬂex wheel and the back(near to center) \nﬂex wheel is the opposite direction. Therefore, the inner \nﬂex wheel would need a gear to change its rotation. \nPennlaine\nPrototype\n128 8/18", "Selecting Solution\nExplanation:\nWhen the piston is extended, the robot will score at the \nlong goal.\nIt would go the exact same route as when the piston is \nretracted. But now the lower ﬂex wheel is more \nimportant as it guides the block up to the outake part. \nAnd ﬁnally, the roof for the shooter part is the blue \ncurve, which will be a plastic board, probably with \nanti-slip mats sticked to it. \nPennlaine 8/18\nPrototype\n129", "Building Solution\nAs we have developed a solid plan of our robot, our \nchassis is also built with our new anoidized parts. \nPennlaine\nBuilding\n130 8/20", "Selecting Solution\n1. We found out that our ﬁrst sprocket would extend \nover size limit if the front channel (light blue) were \nplaced upright, so we tilted it\n2. 2 intake sprockets are parallel to the ground (dark \nblue) laterally\n3. back sprocket has equal space from its ground → \nplastic board(blue) → roof\nPennlaine\nCAD\n131 8/21", "Selecting Solution\n1. We added the ﬁrst sprocket and the second sprocket.\n2. We decided that we would put a 11W motor on the \nsecond sprocket so we had to use another c-channel \nto extend it since motors can’t be on 1*1 angles\n3. We decided that we leave the aligner and build it \nphysically so that we can test the height \nPennlaine\nCAD\n132 8/21", "Selecting Solution\n1. We started to design the shooter , which had to make \nsure the gears wouldn’t touch the ball. \n2. We changed the gear ratio to 1:2, and the motor \nwould be connected to the rotating axis of the \nshooter , so that it wouldn’t be moving while the piston \nis extended. But we haven’t thought of where to put \nthe motor , it would probably be like the lower one, on \nan extended c-channel\nPennlaine\nCAD\n133 8/22", "Selecting Solution\nThese are some more pictures in different angles of \nour CAD because it’s kind of hard to understand it with \none viewing angle.\nPennlaine\nCAD\n134 8/23", "Selecting Solution Pennlaine\n1. As previously mentioned, the red arrow ﬂex wheel has to \nbe rotating the opposite direction of the 2 yellow arrow \nﬂex wheels. \nSo we choosed to use gears \nfor the red arrow ﬂex wheels, \nsince it is adjacent and we \ndecided to put the motor \nconnected to the red 36T gear. \nCAD\n135 8/25", "Selecting Solution Pennlaine\nAnd next we have to chain the motor to the other 2 \nyellow arrow ﬂex wheels, but we can’t just chain \ntogether , we have to chain one by one or it would \ninteract with other structure. (The highlighted red \nsprockets.) ↓↓↓\nCAD\n136 8/25", "Selecting Solution Pennlaine\nWe chained other ﬂex wheels with sprockets and chains.\nThe circles represent chains, so the upper ﬂex wheel is \nchain with both the motor connected axis and the lower \nﬂex wheel axis.\n137\nCAD\n8/25", "Selecting Solution Pennlaine\nThe CAD now looks really complicated and confusing, \nsince the sprockets look all together. However , we think \nthat we can start building, and obviously, we would have \nto change some dimensions when testing during building. \nWe added the ﬂex wheels and the robot is mostly ﬁnished.\n138\nCAD\n8/28", "Selecting Solution Pennlaine\nThis is the ﬁnal CAD, we still have to test:\n1. height of the piston for both retracted and extended\n2. Some spaces  between sprockets might need slight \nadjustments.\n3. shooter motor placement\n4. aligner\n5. tongue\n6. plastic boards and mat placement\n7 .overall intake smoothnest\n139 8/30", "Project Management Jefferson 8/11\n2025/08/30 — 30 minutes\nIn today’s meeting, we \n1. catch up with each others’ progress and conﬁrmed \nthe current CAD design of our robot\n2. assign tasks to each team member\nMeeting\nWho is here Goals for today’s meeting\n- Pennlaine\n- Adam\n- Anthony\n- Jefferson\n- CAD confirmation\n- Building Plan\n140", "Project Management Jefferson 8/30\nBuilding Timeline\n141\nTasks 8/30-\n9/6\n9/7-\n9/15\n9/16-\n9/22\nChassis \n \nIntake \nAligner \nTongue \nColor Sorting \nProgramming \nT asks per member:\n- Chassis: Pennlaine, Adam\n- Intake: Anthony, Jefferson\n- Aligner: Pennlaine, Adam\n- T ongue: Anthony Jefferson\n- Color Sorting: Pennlaine\n- Programming: Pennlaine, Adam", "Building Solution\nAs we have our CAD ﬁnished, we started building. \nPennlaine\nBuilding\n142 9/4", "Selecting Solution\nAfter having built our ﬁrst intake sprocket, we would \nbuild the tongue mech to test if the sprocket could \nsmoothly load with the tongue guiding it. \n75088A demonstrates the meta of match loading, which \nis connected to pistons to extend out and match load.\nPennlaine\nTongue\n143\n 9/8", "Testing Solution\nWe tested out and adjusted the mech to a suitable and \nefficient angle. Then we attacheted it to the bot and \ntested with the intake sprocket. \nPennlaine\nTongue\n144\n 9/8", "Identify the problem\nAt the same time, we started testing the aligner. \nHowever , as the image shows, the robot is too high, it \ncannot go under the long goal.\nPennlaine\nAligner\n145 9/9", "Building Solution\nTherefore, we altered it’s height from 20 holes to 16\n       holes, so that it is just\n below the long goal.            \n  Now that the   \n dimensions, height, \n        width are accurate and\n       passes inspection, we \n       started add sprockets \nand the whole process went really fast. \nWe also add mats for the \nﬂoor of the lower intake\nsystem. The motors act\nas perfect support for the\nmat just as we thought.\nPennlaine\nAlterations\n146 9/9", "Identify the problem\nSince we didn’t CAD our aligner , we are now testing its \nsize physically. \nThe problem is that the aligner would have to both ﬁt \nin the loader tube and the triangular long goal column. \nBoth the width and the depth has to ﬁt in. \nPennlaine\nAligner\n147\n 9/12", "Testing Solution\nSince we don’t have a cnc or laser cutter , we have to \nuse 3d printing for testing and prototyping and then \nask our friend from a frc team to help cut it with their \nschool’s laser cutter. \nPennlaine\nAligner\n148 9/12", "Identify the problem\nProblem 1:\nAligner has to move forward when scoring at long goal \nor else the whole bot would be too near the long goal. \nProblem 2:\nHowever , when the robot is loading at the loaders, it \nneeds to be nearer to the loaders \nPennlaine\nAligner\n149\n 9/12", "Testing Solution\nWe added pistons on the aligner to match the depth.\nPennlaine\nAligner\n150\n 9/12", "Testing Solution\nWe could incorporate color sorting at the end of the \nbot like 7121B BLITZ. So we added pistons at the back of \nthe bot. We would also need to add an optical sensor.\nPennlaine\nRobot 1  COLOR SORTING\n151\n 9/12", "Testing Solution\nWe are testing if the color sorting mech hardware could \nwork, by cooperating with software. \nTEST 1\nT o verify sensor detection reliability under match \nconditions.\nT est Procedure\n1. Place robot in autonomous mode\n2. Feed blocks individually\n3. Record sorting accuracy\n4. Repeat for 4 sets each 5 trials\nT esting sets (T o simulate real matches incidents)\nSet 1: Sort out BLUE - Red, Red, Blue, Red\nSet 2: Sort out BLUE - Blue, Blue, Blue, Red\nSet 3: Sort out RED - Red, Blue, Red, Blue\nSet 4: Sort out RED - Blue, Blue, Blue, Blue\nPennlaine\nColor Sorting\n152 9/13", "Testing Solution\nTEST 1\nThis is the code snippet that we used to test:\nPennlaine\nColor Sorting\n153\nwhile (!Controller1.ButtonB.pressing()) {\n   intake.spin(forward, 12, volt);\n   intakedown.spin(forward, 12, volt);\n   if (Optical.isNearObject()) {\n     vex ::color detectedColor = Optical.color();\n     blockCount++;\n     bool shouldSort = false;\n     if (selectedTeamColor == vex::color::red && \ndetectedColor == vex::color::blue) {\n       shouldSort = true;  // Want red, sort blue\n     } else if (selectedTeamColor == vex::color::blue && \ndetectedColor == vex::color::red) {\n       shouldSort = true;  // Want blue, sort red\n     }\n    \n     if (shouldSort) {\n       pushCylinder = true;\n       sortCount++;\n       wait(0.2, sec);\n       pushCylinder = false;\n     }    \n     // Wait for block to pass\n     while (Optical.isNearObject() && \n!Controller1.ButtonB.pressing()) {\n       wait(20, msec);\n     }\n   }\n9/13", "Testing Solution\nTEST 1\nResults\nProblem\nFrom the testing results, we learned that:\n1. the Optical Sensor has lower sensitivity for blue \ncolors\n2. It might delay when the wrong block is the ﬁrst block \nto sort out. \nPennlaine\nColor Sorting\n154\nSet T arget Correct Sorts Incorrect Accuracy\n1 Blue 4 / 5 Missed 60%\n2 Blue 4 / 5 Didnt stop \nsorting 80%\n3 Red 4 / 5 Missed 1st \nRed 80%\n4 Red 5 / 5 100%\n9/13", "Testing Solution\nAfter identifying the problems, we:\n1. Changed the program logic to NOT RED\n2. Moved the Optical Sensor Forward. \nTEST 2\nUpdated Program Snippet:\nAnalysis\n1. The accuracy improved signiﬁcantly, and we are \nsatisﬁed with our color sort mech.\nPennlaine\nColor Sorting\n155\nSet T arget Correct Sorts Incorrect Accuracy\n1 Blue 5 / 5 Missed 80%\n2 Blue 4 / 5 100%\n3 Red 5 / 5 100%\n4 Red 5 / 5 100%\nif (selectedTeamColor == vex::color::red && (detectedColor \n!= vex::color::red || Optical.hue()<50)) { //Red is 20-40\n       shouldSort = true;  // Want red, sort blue\n     }\n9/13", "Brainstorming \nRecently on VEX’s Discord server , we were inspired by \nthe new meta of match loading tongue introduced by \n338A Iterated. \nPennlaine\nTongue\n156 9/8\n", "Testing Solution\nWe saw the new meta tongue, so we tested both.\nTEST 1\nT o compare loading efficiency between the standard \ntwo-standoff intake interface and a custom plastic \nboard guide under match conditions.\nT est Procedure\n1. Position robot at loader\n2. Align intake with loader opening\n3. Start intake and timer simultaneously\n4. Allow 6 blocks to load\n5. Stop timer when ﬁnal block is secured\nPennlaine\nTongue\n157 9/8\nT rial Standoffs Plastic\n1 5.32 3.91\n2 4.95 3.78\n3 4.71 3.85\n4 4.88 3.73\n5 4.90 3.80\n6 5.03 3.07\n7 4.76 2.97\n8 5.62 3.12", "Testing Solution\nTEST 1\nAvg Loading Time \nBelow plot shows TEST 1 results:\nPennlaine\nTongue\n158 9/8\nConﬁguration Avg Time (s)\nStandoffs 5.02\nPlastic Board 3.53\n", "Testing Solution\nTEST 1\nAnalysis\nThe plastic board guide provided continuous surface \ncontact, reducing lateral block movement and \nminimizing alignment corrections. In contrast, the \nstandoff design allowed blocks to shift between \ncontact points, increasing loading time.\nDecision\nDue to signiﬁcantly reduced loading time and \nimproved guiding consistency, the plastic board \ninterface was selected over the standard standoff \ndesign.\nPennlaine\nTongue\n159 9/8\n", "Building Solution Pennlaine\nPneumatic System\nShooter\nBlock\nInput\nT Fittings \n276-8638\nT Fittings \n276-8638\nT Fittings \n276-8638\nDouble Acting Solenoid\n276-8752\nT Fittings \n276-8638\n50mm Stroke \n276-8644\n75mm Stroke \n276-8646\n75mm Stroke \n276-8646\n160\nShut-off Valve \n276-8755\nT Fittings \n276-8638\n50mm Stroke \n276-8644\n9/16\nPneumatic System Diagram:", "Building Solution Pennlaine\nAligner Color Sort\nT Fittings \n276-8638\nDouble Acting Solenoid\n276-8752\nT Fittings \n276-8638\nT Fittings \n276-8638\n25mm Stroke \n276-8642\n75mm Stroke \n276-8646\n75mm Stroke \n276-8646\n161\n25mm Stroke \n276-8642\nPneumatic System\n9/16", "Project Management\nThe robot is now ﬁnished, we have the upcoming AST \nScrimmage so we really shall start coding autos. At \nleast we have already tried our new programming \nsystem and make sure it works. \nPennlaine\nMeeting  Programming\n162 9/15\nWho is here Goals for today’s meeting\n- Pennlaine\n- Adam\n- Anthony\n- Jefferson \n- Programming Plan\n2025/09/15 — 45 minutes", "Selecting Solution\nCable Management\nJefferson\nThe wires were changed ports many times during our \nbuilding and testing processes for a better / more \nneater organization.\nR1 Motor\nR2 Motor\nR3 Motor\nUpper \nIntake\nL1 Motor\nL2 Motor\nL3 Motor\nLower \nINtake\n163 9/5", "Brainstorming \nController Functions\nJefferson\n- The programmer discussed with driver by \nbrainstorming the required functions:\n- \n1. Motor groups on Drive\na. T ank Drive\nb. \n2. Lower Intake Belt System\na. Forward (pressing)\nb.Reverse (pressing)\nc. Stop (not pressing)\nd. \n3. Upper Intake Belt System\na. Forward (pressing)\nb.Reverse (pressing)\nc. Stop (not pressing)\n164 9/5", "Selecting Solution Jefferson\nProgramming Intro\n165\nIn the past two seasons, we have always used the \nVexCode Pro V5 environment for coding, but last \nseason, during the ﬁnal event, we talked with another \nteam, and they shared that they used a system \n“LemLib” for autonomous coding and ﬁnd it relatively \neasy and efficient.\n9/5", "Selecting Solution Jefferson\nLemLib is an open source VEX coding system that \nhelps code for the VEX robotics drivetrain setup and \nautonomous movements using PROS syntax. It \nprovides the code for the path we drew on the UI.\nThe image provided is an illustration of a possible solo \nAWP path.\n166\nProgramming Intro\n9/5", "Building Solution Jefferson\nThus, in order to have the lemlib and pros syntax \npackages, we cloned the LemLib github repo \n(https://github.com/LemLib/LemLib.git).\nWe really wished that we knew this earlier , because \nworking with VSCode is way easier than we thought. \nAlso, this allowed different programmers to share the \ncode through push and pulling from a mutual github \nrepo, unlike that we had to email it back and forward \nbefore. \nTherefore, we created a github organization of our \nschool and our vex organization team number — \n“23083 Wego Robotics.” Our cloned LemLib ﬁles was our \nﬁrst commit to our newly created repo in our \norganization. \nSetting Up\n167\n 9/5", "Brainstorming \nController Functions\nJefferson\nLower Intake \nReverse\n(Stop when release)\nLower Intake \nForward\n(Stop when release)\nLeft Motor \nGroup\nUpper Intake \nReverse\n(Stop when release)\nUpper Intake \nForward\n(Stop when release)\nRight Motor \nGroup\n168 9/6", "Building Solution\nProgramming\nJefferson\nBasic setup:\nDrive (within the `opcontrol(void)` function):\n   while (true) {\n       // get joystick positions\n       int leftY = \ncontroller.get_analog(pros::E_CONTROLLER_ANALOG_LEFT_Y);\n       int rightX = \ncontroller.get_analog(pros::E_CONTROLLER_ANALOG_RIGHT_Y);\n       // move the chassis with tank drive\n       chassis.tank(leftY, rightY);\n       pros::delay(10);\n       //--------- functions-------------\n       IntakeChain1();\n       IntakeChain2();\n   } \n// controller\npros::Controller controller(pros::E_CONTROLLER_MASTER);\n// Motor Groups\npros::MotorGroup leftMotors({-2, -3, 1},\n                           pros::MotorGearset::blue); \npros::MotorGroup rightMotors({9, 8, -7}, \npros::MotorGearset::blue); \n// Function Motors\npros::Motor Intake1(-11); //lower\npros::Motor Intake2(-5);// upper\n169 9/6", "Building Solution Jefferson\nDrive (within the `IntakeChain1()` function):\nvoid IntakeChain1() { //pressing button function\n   Intake1.set_brake_mode(pros::E_MOTOR_BRAKE_COAST);\n   bool lowerIntake1 = \ncontroller.get_digital(pros::E_CONTROLLER_DIGITAL_L1);\n   bool lowerIntake2 = \ncontroller.get_digital(pros::E_CONTROLLER_DIGITAL_L2);\n   if (lowerIntake1) {\n       Intake1.move_velocity(200);    // L1 forward\n   } \n   else if (lowerIntake2) {\n       Intake1.move_velocity(-200);   // L2 reverse\n   } \n   else {\n       Intake1.brake();\n   } \n}\n170\nIntake Function\n9/7", "Building Solution Jefferson\nDrive (within the `IntakeChain2( )` function):\nvoid IntakeChain2() { //pressing button function\n   Intake2.set_brake_mode(pros::E_MOTOR_BRAKE_COAST);\n   bool upperIntake1 = \ncontroller.get_digital(pros::E_CONTROLLER_DIGITAL_R1);\n   bool upperIntake2 = \ncontroller.get_digital(pros::E_CONTROLLER_DIGITAL_R2);\n   if (upperIntake1) {\n       Intake2.move_velocity(200);    // R1 forward\n   } \n   else if (upperIntake2) {\n       Intake2.move_velocity(-200);   // R2 reverse\n   } \n   else {\n       Intake2.brake();              \n   } \n}\nIntake Function\n171 9/7", "Identify the problem Jefferson\nThe driver mentioned that he would like the drivetrain \nto brake with “coast”.\nWe found that the “tank()” function was within the lemlib \npackage and we did not know how to change it since it \nseemed like an installed precompiled binary, not \nsource code.\nDrivetrain\n172 9/8", "Programming Solution Jefferson\nThe programmer wrote the tank drive manually, setting \nthe individual drivetrain motor’s brake mode as \nE_MOTOR_BRAKE_COAST\nDrivetrain\n173\n// motors set coast\nint deadband(int input, int threshold = 5) {\n   return (abs(input) > threshold) ? input : 0;\n}\ndouble to_volt(int power) {\n   return (power / 127.0) * 12.0;\n}\n9/8\nDrivetrain", "Programming Solution Jefferson\nvoid control_tank(float percentage = 100.0f) {\nleftMotors.set_brake_mode(pros::MotorBrake::coast);\nrightMotors.set_brake_mode(pros::MotorBrake::coast);\n   int leftInput = \ndeadband(pros::Controller(pros::E_CONTROLLER_MASTER).get_analog(pros\n::E_CONTROLLER_ANALOG_LEFT_Y));\n   int rightInput = \ndeadband(pros::Controller(pros::E_CONTROLLER_MASTER).get_analog(pros\n::E_CONTROLLER_ANALOG_RIGHT_Y));\n   double leftVoltage = to_volt(leftInput * percentage / 100.0);\n   double rightVoltage = to_volt(rightInput * percentage / 100.0);\n   if (leftVoltage != 0) leftMotors.move_voltage(leftVoltage * \n1000);\n   else {\n       left1.brake();\n       left2.brake();\n       left3.brake();\n   } \n   if (rightVoltage != 0) rightMotors.move_voltage(rightVoltage * \n1000);\n   else {\n       right1.brake();\n       right2.brake();\n       right3.brake();\n   } \n}\nDrivetrain\n174 9/8\nDrivetrain", "Identify the problem Pennlaine\nWhile we tried to write our autonomous, we found out \nthat Lemlib has several problems. \n1. The encoder units for each our wheel changes to \ndifferent ones for the Chassis function\n2. It doesn’t really beneﬁt us a lot since we don’t have \nodometry wheels so the copied paths aren’t really \nprecise anyways. \n3. Combining Lemlib with pros has several library or \nversion problems\nDrivetrain\n175 9/9", "Selecting Solution Pennlaine\nTherefore we changed to JAR-T emplate which we found \nmore straight forward, since the library is written all \nwithin the ﬁles not with extensions, and we have more \nﬂexibility with changing it and tailoring it. \n176\nDrivetrain\n9/9", "Programming Solution\nSystem Architecture\nPennlaine\nThe robot runs a modular software setup:\n● Main Control Loop (main.cpp): Handles competition \ninitialization and match management.\n● Drive System (JAR-T emplate): T ank drive with \nodometry, PID control, and motion proﬁling.\n● Autonomous Routines (autons.cpp): Pre-programmed \nwith several routes to choose before match starts.\n● Driver Control: Separate tasks for action and \nsensors.\n● User Interface: T ouchscreen selection and real-time \ndashboard telemetry.\nVEX/\n├── src/\n│   ├── main.cpp           # Competition control, UI, custom \nmovement\n│   ├── robot-config.cpp   # Hardware definitions\n│   ├── autons.cpp         # 10 autonomous routines\n│   ├── arm.cpp            # Hang control task \n│   ├── note.cpp           # Color sorting task \n│   └── JAR-Template/\n│       ├── drive.cpp      # Tank drive implementation\n│       ├── odom.cpp       # Odometry calculations\n│       ├── PID.cpp        # PID controller logic \n│       └── util.cpp       # Helper functions\n├── include/\n│   └── [corresponding .h files]\n└── build/\n    └── Jar-Template.bin   # Compiled binary for V5 Brain\n177 9/10", "Programming Solution Pennlaine\nJAR-T emplate Drive Framework\nFeatures:\n- T ank drive \n- PID controllers for drive, turn, heading, and swing\n- Autonomous point-to-point navigation with \nheading correction\nDrive Conﬁguration\nZERO_TRACKER_ODOM:\n- Uses drive motor encoders + inertial sensor for \nposition tracking\n- No dedicated tracking wheels required\n- Forward tracker offset (5.2\") compensates for \ncenter of rotation\n- Sideways tracker detects lateral drift during turns\nDrivetrain Control\n178 9/11", "Programming Solution Pennlaine\nThe cos_move_distance_smooth() function \nimplements a sophisticated motion proﬁle:\n- Acceleration Phase (22% of distance): Cosine-based \nsmooth acceleration\n- \n- Cruise Phase (37% of distance): Constant velocity at \nVmax)\n- Deceleration Phase (41% of distance): Cosine-based \nsmooth deceleration)\n   v = Vmax × 0.5 × (1 - cos(π × progress))\n   v = Vmax × 0.5 × (1 + cos(π × progress))\nProgramming\n179\n 9/11", "Programming Solution Pennlaine\nHeading Correction:\n- Instead of using all 3 PID values of correction, since \nwe aren’t using odometry, we decided to only use 2 \nfor heading. \n- Proportional Gain (kH = 0.17): Corrects heading error\n- Integral Gain (kHi = 0.003): Eliminates steady-state \nerror\n- Integral Capping (±50% of max): Prevents integral \nwindup\nProgramming\n180\n 9/11", "Programming Solution Pennlaine\nSettling Logic:\n- Detects when within 0.3 inches of target\n- Requires < 0.02 inch/cycle movement for 150ms\n- Prevents overshoot and oscillation\nProgramming\n181\n 9/11", "Programming Solution Pennlaine\nAUTONOMOUS PROGRAMMING\nAutonomous Selection System\n- T ouchscreen selects one of 10 routines\n- Stored in current_auton_selection (0–9)\nR is for red, B is for B. F is autonomous strategy for \nﬁnals, not qualiﬁcations, so that we don’t have to \nconsider AWP. Solo is for solo AWP.\n int previous_selection = -1;\n const int cols      = 5;\n const int screen_w  = 480;\n const int screen_h  = 240;\n const int col_width = screen_w / cols;\n const int red_height  = screen_h / 2 + 6;\n const int blue_height = screen_h - red_height;\n const char* labels[10] = {\n   \"R_right\",\"R_left\",\"R_right_F\",\"R_left_F\",\"R_solo\",\n   \"B_right\",\"B_left\",\"B_right_F\",\"B_left_F\",\"B_solo\"\n };\n Brain.Screen.setFont(vex::fontType::mono20);\nProgramming\n182 9/13", "Programming Solution Pennlaine\nwhile (!auto_started)\n {\n   if (current_auton_selection != previous_selection)\n   {\n     previous_selection = current_auton_selection;\n     for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++)\n     {\n       int col = i % cols;\n       bool top = (i < cols);\n       int x = col * col_width;\n       int y = top ? 0 : red_height;\n       int h = top ? red_height : blue_height;\n       vex ::color fillColor = (current_auton_selection == i) ? white : (top \n? red : blue);\n       vex ::color textColor = (current_auton_selection == i) ? red   : \nwhite;\n       Brain.Screen.setFillColor(fillColor);\n       Brain.Screen.drawRectangle(x, y, col_width, h);\n       Rect r { x, y, col_width, h };\n       drawCenteredText(r, labels[i], textColor);\n     }\n   }   \nWhen competition for the ﬁeld is on, the robot \nautomatically starts as the chosen one. \nProgramming\n183 9/13", "Programming Solution Pennlaine\nif (Brain.Screen.pressing())\n   {\n     int touchX = Brain.Screen.xPosition();\n     int touchY = Brain.Screen.yPosition();\n     int col = touchX / col_width;\n     int row = (touchY < red_height) ? 0 : (touchY < red_height + \nblue_height ? 1 : -1);\n     if (col >= 0 && col < cols && (row == 0 || row == 1)) {\n       current_auton_selection = col + row * cols;\n      \n       // enter next page（Dashboard）\n      \n       show_status_page(current_auton_selection);\n     }\n     wait(0.3, sec);\n   }\n   wait(20, msec);\n }\nThis is the basic UI of the brain for choosing autons \nProgramming\n184 9/13", "Programming Solution Pennlaine\nUser Interface (on the brain)\n1. T ouch a box to select autonomous routine\n2. Selected box turns WHITE with colored text\n3. T ouch again to enter Dashboard Mode\nUser Interface\n185\n 9/13", "Programming Solution Pennlaine\nDashboard Display\nT ab 1: INPUTS\nT ab 2: MOTORS\nDisplays motor positions in real-time:\nColor-coded elements (blue = selected, green = active)\nUser Interface\n186\n 9/14", "Programming Solution Pennlaine\nPID Control T uning\nDefault Constants\nvoid default_constants() {\n   // DRIVE PID\n   chassis.set_drive_constants(12, 0.5, 0.004, 5, 20);\n   //                           ^V  ^kP   ^kI    ^kD  ^startI \n  \n   // HEADING PID (gyro correction during drive)\n   chassis.set_heading_constants(12, 0.25, 0.007, 5, 40);\n  \n   // TURN PID\n   chassis.set_turn_constants(12, 0.35, 0.001, 3, 90);\n  \n   // SWING PID (one-side pivot)\n   chassis.set_swing_constants(12, 0.5, 0.001, 2, 15);\n  \n   // EXIT CONDITIONS\n   chassis.set_drive_exit_conditions(0.5, 0, 2000);  // ±0.5\" error, \n2s timeout\n   chassis.set_turn_exit_conditions(4, 0, 3000);     // ±4° error, \n3s timeout\n}\nPID\n187 9/14", "Programming Solution Pennlaine\nPID Parameter Meanings\nParamete\nr Value Effect T uning Guide\nkP 0.5–2.0 Aggressiveness \nof correction\nHigher = faster \nresponse, \nmore \novershoot\nkI 0.001–0.0\n07\nEliminates \nsteady-state \nerror\nT oo high = \noscillation\nkD 2–5 Damping/smoo\nthness\nHigher = more \nstable, slower\nstartI 15–90\nError threshold \nto begin \nintegral\nPrevents \nwindup at \nlarge errors\nPID\n188 9/14", "Programming Solution\nAutonomous Strategy\nPennlaine\nAutonomous Strategy\n189 9/15", "Programming Solution\nSimulator\nAdam\nBecause our school limits our time to practice at our \nrobotics classroom, combined with the fact that after \nwe build our car we are mostly using time for \nprogramming, our drivers always say they don’t have \nenough time to practice driving and think of new \nstrategies. T o allow drivers to practice effectively, we \ndeveloped a 2D virtual simulator using HTML. \n9/5190", "Programming Solution\nSimulator\nAnthony\nThe ﬁrst prototype of our simulation. It looks nothing \nlike the real ﬁeld as of now as we’re still experimenting \nwhat works and what doesn’t. We added a few goals \nand expanded the ﬁeld just to test the mechanics. So \nfar it covers the essentials: customizable robot \nvariables, automatic intakes, and ball spawning. One \nmajor issue we faced was the object hitboxes. As we \ncoded on HTML instead of a game engine like Unity, \nwe have to make one from scratch. We started with \nAABB for it is lightweight and cheap, but it doesn’t \nalways yield the desire results.  \n9/5\n191", "Programming Solution\nSimulator\nAnthony\nThe problem with AABB is that it is essentially four \ndirectional. T op, bottom, left, and right are all of its \ndirections. But robots rotate. If we had to use AABB, the \nbest way to do it would be to ues multiple boxes, like \nminecraft. But using multiple boxes kills its purpose: \nthat its cheap and easy to do. Even with using multiple \nboxes, the hitbox is still going to look something like \nthis(red).\n9/5192\n", "Programming Solution\nSimulator\nAnthony\nThis hitbox  creates a false vision where the bot \nrealistically should be touching something but isn’t. T o \nadd salt to the injury, it is horrendously bad with \ncircles. Which will clash with our later ideas of block \nphysics. Where hitboxes should be met with a circular \nface would be pushed back by a ﬂat one, and throws \noff the physics engine completely.\n9/5193\n", "Programming Solution\nSimulator\nAnthony\n The second version of the game. This version brought \nthe ﬁeld much closer to the actual ﬁeld compared to \nthe last. We decided to keep the ﬁeld rectangular since \na square setup feels too cramped and limits testing \nspace. We also introduced team colors - red and blue - \ninstead of the original yellow to better match real \nmatch conditions. We opted for the game to randomly \nspawn balls rather than ﬁxed positions at the start,  \nallowing  continuous practice without having to restart \nthe simulation.\n9/9\n194", "Programming Solution\nSimulator\nAnthony\nWe changed from autonomous intake to manual as \nit more closely represent real match conditions. The \nsky blue box in the front is the indicator for the area \nfor which balls can be collected, and the green lights \nindicates that intake is active.\nOn the interface side, we also enhanced the player’s \nability to track performance with this new stats panel. \nIt displays real time statistics like the current score, \nnumber of balls stored in the robots, and other \nessential data. \n9/9\n195", "Programming Solution\nSimulator\nAnthony 9/9\nWe introduced several key elements to better \nsimulate competitive match conditions. Players can \nnow enable a two player mode, which allows players to \nplug in dual controllers for head-to-head competition. \nA much more enticing prospect than solo practice.\nAdditionally, players can now \nchoose between tank drive or \narcade drive to match their preferred style or practice \ntactics used by other teams. But it’s for both robots \nright now.\nWhile testing, we realized camping at loaders takes \nall the fun away from the game. So we added the \noption to disable loaders, to prevent camping and \ninﬁnite scoring tactics.\n196", "Programming Solution\nSimulator\nAnthony 9/11\nFor the hitbox, we ﬁgured out how the OBB method \nworks and successfully coded a way to make balls \nbounce off the robot when not collected. You can also \npush robots that are scoring off the goal just like real \nlife situations. A bar for customizing object’s push \nstrength was added to adapt to different robots.\n- Converting top-left coordinate system to \ncenter-based system with rotation\nfunction aabbToObb(aabb){\n  return{\n    cx: aabb.x + aabb.w/2, cy: aabb.y + aabb.h/2, hx: \naabb.w/2, hy: aabb.h/2, angle:0\n  }; \n}\nWe converted to center-based coordinates becuase \nthe rotations doesn’t rotate around corners and it \nsimpliﬁes calculations.\n197", "Programming Solution\nSimulator\nAnthony 9/11\nT o understand OBB, we must ﬁrst glance at its \nunderlying theory: the Seperation Axis Theory (SAT). \nUsing simple maths we can check if the boxes overlap. \nBy checking if the top of A overlaps the bottom of B \nand vice versa, we can see there is an overlap between \ntopB and bottomA. But, the theory states if there is a \npossible way that the objects don’t overlap, it doesn’t. \nNow take a look at the right and left. The x axis doesn’t \noverlap in this case. Hence these boxes don’t collide.\n198\n", "Programming Solution\nSimulator\nAnthony 9/11\nBut what if the objects rotated? How do you determine \nits top and bottom? Great ask. In such situations, we \nﬁnd the maximum and minimum point of the object’s x \nand y axis, and then check for overlapping. If we can \ndraw a line that separates the two boxes, the objects \ndon’t collide, as illustrated below.\n199\n", "Programming Solution\nSimulator\nAnthony 9/11\nFor any convex polygon, the only direction one needs \nto check for separation are the ones perpendicular to \neach of its edges. Our OBB (a rectangle) has four \nedges, but the opposite edges are parallel and share \nthe same perpendicular direction. Therefore, each OBB \nonly contributes to 2 unique axes. Since we have 2 \nOBBs (A and B), we need to check 4 axes. These \nlocations are simply the X and Y directions of the \nrobot, which are calculated by using the sin and cosine \nof the robots rotation angle.\nfunction obbObbPenetration(A, B){\n  const axes = [];\n  const aAx = { x: Math.cos(A.angle), y: Math.sin(A.angle)};\n  const aAy = { x: -Math.sin(A.angle), y: Math.cos(A.angle)};\n  const bAx = { x: Math.cos(B.angle), y: Math.sin(B.angle)};\n  const bAy = { x: -Math.sin(B.angle), y: Math.cos(B.angle)};\n  axes.push(aAx,aAy,bAx,bAy);\n200", "Programming Solution\nSimulator\nAnthony 9/11\nOnce we have an axis to test, we project both OBBs on \nit. It’s like shining a light directly onto our rectangle, the \nshadow casts by the boxes represents its projection. If \nthe shadows don’t overlap then the robots don’t \nintersect. \nMathematically speaking, these projections are found \nby using the dot product between each vertex of the \nrobot and the direction of the axis. Normally you’ll \nhave to check all the corners and ﬁnd the largest and \nsmallest points. \n201\n", "Programming Solution\nSimulator\nAnthony 9/11\nCode that ﬁrst calculates if the boxes are overlapping, \nthen it determines which direction the box should push \nsomething.\nlet minOverlap = Infinity;\n  let smallestAxis = null;\n  let axisSign = 1;\n  for(const ax of axes){\n    const projA = projectObbOnAxis(A, ax.x, ax.y);\n    const projB = projectObbOnAxis(B, ax.x, ax.y);\n    const overlap = Math.min(projA.max, projB.max) - \nMath.max(projA.min, projB.min);\n    if(overlap <= 0) return null;\n    if(overlap < minOverlap){\n      minOverlap = overlap;\n      smallestAxis = { x: ax.x, y: ax.y};\n      const dirX = A.cx - B.cx, dirY = A.cy - B.cy;\n      const dot = dirX*ax.x + dirY*ax.y;\n      axisSign = ( dot < 0) ? -1 : 1;\n      } \n    } return {\n      px: smallestAxis.x * minOverlap * axisSign, py: smallestAxis.y * \nminOverlap * axisSign, penetration: minOverlap\n    } \n  } \n202", "Programming Solution\nSimulator\nAnthony 9/11\nContinuing from the last page, it is not enough if \nwe only know how far the box should move, we also \nneed to know which direction the box should move. \nT o ﬁgure this out, we look at the vector that goes \nfrom center of  box B to box A, this is basically just a \nline that points from one box to another. We then \ncompare  the same vector with the axis that we’re \ncurrently using to push the box. \nBy taking the dot product we can know if the vector \nis pointing towards the same direction or the opposite. \nThis gives us a +1 or -1 depending on the direction, \nensuring that box A is always pushing away from box B.\nconst dirX = A.cx - B.cx, dirY = A.cy - B.cy;\n      const dot = dirX*ax.x + dirY*ax.y;\n      axisSign = ( dot < 0) ? -1 : 1;\n203", "Programming Solution\nSimulator\nAnthony 9/11\nFinal version of the simulator. We shrinked \neverything to ﬁt the square ﬁeld, which better \nrepresents the actual thing. We also ﬁxed the problem \nwhere robots can’t ues different drive schemes. So now \nplayers can choose between tank drive and arcade \ndrive based on their preference. Overall, we polished \nevery one of our functions to make the calculations \nmore accurate and make the game run smoother.\n204", "Programming Solution Adam\n● Drivers can practice at \nanywhere with our \nsimulator.\n● We believe we’re the ﬁrst \nto make one in this \nseason.\nField Setup Timed like real matches\nCustomized robot \ndimensions\nCustomized robot drivetrain \nto simulate our own robot\nSimulator\n205 9/11", "Programming Solution Adam\nOverall, our simulator features:\n● Compatibility with vex controllers\n● A 2-player battle mode for practice\n● 2D block collision physics for realistic interactions\n● Accurate scaling of the ﬁeld, objects, and our robot\n● Scoring mechanisms for both middle and long goals\n● Functional loaders for ball manipulation\n● Precise driving timers for performance tracking\n● Mode switching between tank drive and arcade drive\n● Customizable robot features for opposing alliances\n9/11\nSimulator\n206", "Project Management Pennlaine 9/18\nStorage Item Qty ✅\n Packed\nBig Box Robot 1 ☐\n T eam \nlicense \nplates\n4 per team ☐\n Controllers 1+ spare ☐\nLarge Red \nBox\nAir pump 2 ☐\n Batteries 4 + all spares ☐\n Battery \ncharger\n1 + all spares ☐\n Grinder 1 ☐\n Grinder \nbatteries\n2 ☐\n Label maker 1 (in box) ☐\n Safety \ngoggles\n9 ☐\n Brain + \ncontroller\n1+ spare set ☐\n207\nWe are listing out what we should pack up for every \nevent. ", "Project Management Pennlaine 9/18\nStorage Item Qty ✅\n Packed\nBottom \nDrawer\nScrewdrivers 7 ☐\nSmall green tools all ☐\nWrenches all ☐\nNeedle-nose pliers 2 ☐\nDiagonal cutters 2 ☐\nPliers 1 ☐\nSmall & large L hex \nwrenches\nfor \nscrews\n☐\nGreen wire \ncrimping clips\nall ☐\nZip ties all ☐\nRubber bands all ☐\nT op \nDrawer\nRubber wheels 10 ☐\nT op \nDrawer\nRed sprockets 16 24T ☐\n208", "Project Management Pennlaine 9/18\nStorage Item Qty ✅\n Packed\nPneumati\nc Box\nT-connectors full bag ☐\n Switches a few ☐\n 90° elbow ﬁttings extra ☐\n Solenoid 4+ ☐\n Air tanks 2 ☐\n Air tubing several \nrolls\n☐\n T ubing cutter 1 ☐\n Old solenoid cables 2→3, no \nblue\n☐\n New cylinders all ☐\nOther Red \nBoxes\nstandoffs all ☐\n Screws all ☐\n Shaft collars & \nspacers\nall ☐\n209", "Competition Analysis\nTAI VEX PRACTICE\nCecilia210\nMATCH RESULTS\nP2 23083Z\n66799X 30 9 89089Z\n96969W\nQ5 23083Z\n66994D 66 11 23083G\n66995A\nQ11 23083Z\n17022J 80 3 3866F\n96969Y\nQ17 66996X\n66995W 14 5 23083Z\n66995A\nQ22 10004B\n10004N 25 50 23083Z\n10004X\nQ30 23083Z\n17022X 30 50 89089Y\n17022A\nQ35 23083Y\n1791V 63 28 66799X\n23083Z\nR16 \n6-1\n23083Z\n96969W 31 43 10004B\n66799X\n9/28", "Competition Analysis\nTAI VEX practice\nWe had our very ﬁrst match in P2. From this game, we \ntook notice of how crucial autonomous phase is in this \nseason. Both alliances scored minimally throughout \nthe match, but our victory ultimately came down to the \ntwo balls we successfully scored in the middle goal \nduring the autonomous phase.\nAdam\n211\n23083Z\n66799X 30 9 89089Z\n96969W\n9/28", "Competition Analysis\nAlthough Q5 wasn’t our most efficient performance, it \nresulted in a win thanks to solid pre-match \ncoordination. Our alliance planned for 66994D to \nprioritize the long goal while our robot focused on \napplying pressure and collecting points from the \nmiddle goals. While our execution fell short in scoring \noutput, the strategy still proved effective in maintaining \noverall control of the match.\nAdam\n212\nTAI VEX practice\n23083Z\n66994D 66 11 23083G\n66995A\n9/28", "Competition Analysis\nMatch Q11 provided a valuable learning experience for our \nteam. We stciked to our established strategy, maintaining a \ndivision of roles in which our alliance partner prioritized scoring \nwhile our robot focused on defensive maneuvers. Early in the \nmatch, one of our opposing robots became immobilized, \nshifting the competitive dynamic signiﬁcantly. As a result, our \ndefensive efforts were concentrated on countering team \n96969Y’s offensive plays. Unfortunately, during one of these \ndefensive interactions, our robot made unintended contact that \ncaused the opposing robot to tip over , leading to our \ndisqualiﬁcation.\nAdam\n213\nTAI VEX practice\n23083Z\n17022J 80 3 3866F\n96969Y\n9/28", "Competition Analysis\nMatch Q22 follows the same strategy, securing another \nwin. Although there was a minor conﬂict regarding \nentanglement, our team wasn’t penalized.\nAdam\n214\nTAI VEX practice\n10004B\n10004N 25 50 23083Z\n10004X\n9/28", "Competition Analysis\nMatch Q30 presented an interesting dynamic, as both our alliance \nand one of our opponents ﬁelded nearly identical robot designs \n(17022X and 17022A). Our strategy centered on applying sustained \ndefensive pressure, which proved effective for the most part. We \nsuccessfully neutralized 17022A, preventing them from scoring \nthroughout most of the match. However , our early loss in the \nautonomous phase placed us at a scoring disadvantage from the \nstart, which we failed to fully compensate for during driver control. \nAdditionally, our robot wasn’t good for double parking. Had we \nexecuted a double parking in the endgame, we likely would have \nsecured a narrow victory.\nAdam\n215\nTAI VEX practice\n23083Z\n17022X 30 50 89089Y\n17022A\n9/28", "Competition Analysis\nMatch R16 #6-1 unfortunately marked the end of our run in this \ncompetition. We adjusted our strategy for this round, as our \nalliance partner also excelled in rapid cycling and defensive \nplay. Consequently, our role shifted toward primary scoring. Our \nperformance, however , fell short of expectations. This is partly \ndue to intermittent intake lag. In hindsight, we realized that we \ncould have capitalized on the cleared-out tube during the ﬁnal \nmoments of the match, as our alliance’s strong defensive \ncoverage provided a strategic opening. Instead, we focused too \nheavily on reclaiming the middle goal bonus, which ultimately \ncost us valuable points.\nAdam\n216\nTAI VEX practice\n23083Z\n96969W 31 43 10004B\n66799X\n9/28", "Competition Analysis\nOptimal Alliance\nUpon analyzing our performance in TAI-VEX, we came \nto the conclusion that our bot’s archetype is well-ﬁtted \nin the guerilla-supportive role. Our bot’s agiability and \nfast cycling nature allows us to inﬁltrate into \nopponents’ side ﬁeld, sabotaging their game plan while \nchipping scores. Therefore, it’s ideal for us to have an \nalliance that solely focuses on scoring and occupying \nthe long goals. It’s crucial for our alliance to clear out \nloaders and camp at one of the long goals, since our \nrobot’s main limitation lies in its ability to store a large \nnumber of blocks. This, we believe, is the primary cause \nfor us to lose the game in the elimination bracket \nround. \nTypical heavy S-Bot & Basket-Bot\nAdam\n9/30217", "Competition Analysis\nT eam: 10004X\nT ournament: TAI VEX practice\nKey Features:\n● Innovative design with descoring wings\n● Not focused on high speed or large capacity\n● Mechanism specialized for descoring and obstruction\nStrategy & Performance:\n● Used wings for last-second descores\n● Obstructed goals and stalled match tempo effectively\nSummary:\n10004X leverages smart descoring mechanics and tempo \ncontrol to offset its lower scoring speed, making it a \ndeceptively strategic opponent.\nAdam\nAnalysis of \nOthers’ Robots\n218 9/28", "Competition Analysis\nT eam: 66994D\nT ournament: TAI VEX practice\nKey Features:\n● Specialized long-goal control bot\n● Well-engineered loader intake system\n● Plastic stabilizing plate for consistent block intake\n● Reduced risk of blocks exiting the ﬁeld\nStrategy & Performance:\n● Focused on long-goal dominance\n● Provided stable and reliable block control in \nmatches\nSummary:\n66994D is a precise long-goal control bot with a highly \nreliable loader intake that strengthens long-goal \nscoring consistency.\nAdam\nAnalysis of \nOthers’ Robots\n219 9/29", "Competition Analysis\nT eam: 1791V\nT ournament: TAI VEX practice\nKey Features:\n● Typical high-throughput basket bot design\n● Optimized to take advantage of the large block \ncount (88 blocks on Push Back ﬁeld)\n● Integrated color-sorting system to double scoring \nefficiency\nSummary:\n1791V is a high-capacity, quantity-focused basket bot \nwith exceptional scoring potential, making them a \nconsistently dangerous opponent.\nAdam\nAnalysis of \nOthers’ Robots\n220 9/29", "Competition Analysis\nT eam: 17022X, 17022J, and 17022A\nT ournament: TAI VEX practice\nKey Features:\n● Identical fast C-Bot build\n● High-capacity block containment system\n● Two-way-out scoring mechanism\n● Optimized for rapid long-goal scoring\nStrategy & Performance:\n● Prioritized long goals throughout the match\n● Maintained long-goal bonuses almost continuously\n● Filled the entire long goal from the start of the match\n● Relied on high speed and strong accuracy for \ndominance\nAdam\nAnalysis of \nOthers’ Robots\n221 9/29", "Competition Analysis\nSummary:\nThese teams used a fast, precise, and aggressive \nlong-goal strategy that allowed them to consistently \ndominate matches through speed and efficient scoring.\nAdam\nAnalysis of \nOthers’ Robots\n222 9/29", "05\nROBOT 2\n223", "Project Management Pennlaine 9/23\n2025/09/23 — 85 minutes\nCompetition reﬂection:\nAfter participating in the TAI VEX scrimmage last \nweekend, overall, our robot ran smoothly, but there were \nstill some major issues. During a few matches, the robot \nfailed to intake consistently after several collisions, \nwhich slightly lowered our scoring rate. The new \nJAR-T emplate control system worked reliably, and the \ndrive response felt stable.\nSince our robot was ﬁnished quite late, we didn’t have \nenough time to properly test or tune our autonomous \nroutines after verifying basic programming and \nfunctionality. As a result, we went into the event with \nlimited auton reliability. We think this was mainly due to \npoor planning, but also because the competition was \nscheduled early in the season while we were still \nﬁnalizing the design — we always aim to build the best \nrobot possible, which often pushes testing later than \nplanned.\nMeeting Logs\n224\nWho is here Goals for today’s meeting\n- Pennlaine\n- Adam\n- Anthony\n- Jefferson\n- Reflection\n- New robot plan\n", "Project Management Pennlaine 9/23\nRobot performance analysis:\nThe team agreed that the chassis was not stable and \nagile enough for both defense and offense and \nespecially autons. The intake compression was \nsometimes too tight when the ball entered at an angle, \ncausing jams near the second sprocket.\nThe drivers discussed again about curvature vs tank \ndrive — curvature felt smoother when avoiding \nobstacles, but the braking felt unpredictable. \nImprovements planning:\nWe thought that our robot should be changed from its \nchassis since when doing autons it often runs \ndifferently. We think that the chassis should be made \nlooser and smoother. So instead of changing parts by \nparts of the car , we decided to rebuild a similar robot. \nRobot 2 is the bot mentioned above.\nMeeting Logs\n225", "Identify the problem\nAfter our TAI VEX event we realized that our robot had \nmany defects. \n1. Intake \nThe intake often jammed or slipped. This robot needs a \nmore forgiving compression system that can handle \noff-center entries and still maintain smooth cycling.\n This may include adjustable compression spacing, \nmore elastic roller mounting, or wider intake geometry.\n2. Structural Durability\n Several matches revealed that its intake and chassis \nwere slightly too rigid, leading to vibration and \nmisalignment after impacts.\nThe new robot should focus on reinforced yet ﬂexible \nmounting — especially for subsystems that experience \ncollisions (intake arms, side plates, etc.).\nAdam\nReﬂection\n226 9/29", "Identify the problem\n3. Center of Gravity and Stability\n During defense, its higher center of gravity made it \nprone to tilting during collisions or rapid turns.\nThe next design should lower heavy components \n(batteries, pneumatics) and optimize weight \ndistribution across the drivetrain for smoother \nacceleration and balance.\n5. Endgame Capability\n Robot 1 was limited in its ability to double-park quickly, \nwhich cost points in multiple close matches.\n The next robot should integrate a more compact and \nefficient parking mechanism, possibly using a \nlow-proﬁle piston lift or foldable ramp system.\n6. Adaptability in Strategy\n The next robot should have modular autonomous \noptions, allowing it to adapt between offensive and \ndefensive roles depending on alliance needs.\nAdam227\nReﬂection\n9/29", "Project Management Jefferson 8/30\nBuilding Timeline\n228\nTasks 9/29-\n10/6\n10/7-\n10/15\n10/16-\n10/22\nChassis \n \nIntake Design \nSprocket \nRoof \nMat, Plastic Boards, \nDescorer \nProgramming \nT asks per member:\n- Chassis: Pennlaine, Adam\n- Intake design: Anthony, Jefferson\n- Sprocket: Pennlaine, Adam\n- Roof: Anthony \n- Mat, Plastic Boards: Pennlaine, Anthony\n- Programming: Pennlaine, Adam", "Selecting Solution\nWe ﬁnished the CAD of our chassis with new added \ndetails. And we are starting to build the super \nstructure after having brainstormed it.\nAnthony\nChassis CAD\n229 9/6", "Selecting Solution\nWe started to CAD the super structure, like the ﬁrst \nrobot, we start from the ramp with sprockets for a \nseries of consistent intake. \nAnthony\nCAD\n230 9/6\n", "Identify the problem\nWe should adjust the angle of the ramp to that the end \nof the ramp is about the height of the middle upper \ngoal. Similar to Robot 1, we want the second level, the \nshooter part to lie horizontally, to minimize the height \nof the bot, and that exactly score into the the moddle \nupper goal. \nAnthony\nCAD\n231 9/6\n", "Selecting Solution\nWe adjusted the sprockets spacing and added \nstructures to attach motors.\nWe also adjusted the intake sprockets spacing and \nwidths. \nAnthony\nCAD\n232 9/6\n", "Selecting Solution\nWe started to add the second level and align its height.\nWe also maintained that the height was able to go \nunder the long goal. \nAnthony\nCAD\n233 9/6\n", "Selecting Solution\nWe basically ﬁnished the main structure of the robot, \nbut we need testing for the sprockets and heights and \npistons location. Therefore, we decided to start \nbuilding along. \nAnthony\nCAD\n234 9/6\n", "Identify the problem\nAs mentioned, after our TAI VEX event we realized that \nour robot had many defects. So ﬁrst of all, we had to \nchange the chassis. \nInstead of using symmetric spacing for each gears and \nwheels and since we found out that each one would be \nslightly different in its dimensions, we wanted to know if \nwe ﬁlling them with washers and leaving each with a \nwasher wide gap would improve its smoothnest. \nPennlaine\n235\nChassis Spacing\n9/30", "Testing Solution\nWe did an experiment to compare which would be \nsmoother , we built half of a new chassis and used old \nspacings ﬁrst, then switch up to the new spacings. \nTEST 1\nT o measure friction caused by different spacing.\nT est Procedure\n1. Lift robot so wheels are off ground\n2. Run program with drivetrain at 50% power for 3 sec\n3. Cut power instantly and start stopwatch\n4. Stop when wheels stop spinning\nPennlaine236\nChassis Spacing\n9/30", "Testing Solution\nTEST 1\nResults\nAverage Spin Time\nPennlaine237\nChassis Spacing\nT rial Old New\n1 7 .38 10.21\n2 7 .12 10.44\n3 7 .31 10.08\n4 7 .59 10.63\n5 7 .33 10.31\n6 7 .67 10.78\n7 7 .28 10.17\n8 7 .05 10.52\nAvg Old New\n 7 .34 10.39\n9/30", "Selecting Solution\nTEST 1\nBelow plot shows TEST 1 results:\nAnalysis:\nThe washer-spaced conﬁguration produced longer \nfree-spin times, indicating less friction. Controlled \nspacing allowed consistent free-spinning. .\nPennlaine238\nChassis Spacing\n9/30", "Building Solution\nChassis Spacing:\nTherefore, washer spacing was adopted in the ﬁnal \nchassis design.\nAnd we are starting to build our new chassis. \nPennlaine239\nChassis Spacing\n9/30", "Identify the problem\nAfter we have ﬁnished our new chassis and made sure \nit works perfectly, we started to think of the intake \ndesign. We thought that our last bot central idea was \nsolid, the most fatal problem was that both levels of \nintake systems share the same level of sprockets. \nPennlaine\nChassis\n240 10/1", "Selecting Solution Anthony 10/2241\nIntake Design\nTherefore, we decided to build two levels, but this \nmeant that we have to precisely control the height to \nlimit it under the long goal.\nRobot Sketch:\nThe new design features a dual phase shooter system, \nutilizing pneumonics for lifting. Aiming, still, for the \nability to outmaneuver opponents by going through \nlong goals. The roof could use either plastic boards or \nanti-slip cloths as alloys probably won’t do the trick as \ncloths provide better compression.\n", "Selecting Solution Pennlaine 10/2242\nIntake Design\nWe started to test the height of the second level slope.\nHere are some ideas that we came up with:\n1. minimize the robot size → the second level should be \nhorizontal, so that there isn’t a ramped up height.\n2. Considering 1., the lower level should be ramped so \nthat its end is at the middle goal scoring level. \n3. Pistons should be installed so that when extended, it \nscores to the long goal level.\n", "Identify the problem\nWe found out that this height was working but it is \ninterfering with the place we would want to place our \nmotor and sprocket since it was extending over too \nmuch space. \nPennlaine\nIntake Design\n243\n 10/4", "Testing Solution\nTherefore, we changed our pistons’ position and still \nmaintain the same height for both extended and \nretracted. \nPennlaine\nIntake Design\n244\n 10/4", "Building Solution\nWe have started to build our lower level sprockets, \ntrying to let the shafts of the sprockets remain parallel \nto the ﬂoor of the ﬁrst level, which will basically be set \nalong the ramped up angles. \nWe decided to set the 11W motor on the second \nsprocket considering that we could accelerate the \nﬁrst/outermost sprocket using gear ratios. We think \nthat a 2:1 ratio which makes the ﬁrst sprocket intaking \n1200 rpm is pretty solid. \nPennlaine\n245\nIntake Design\n10/5", "Identify the problem\nThe next part of the robot is the most important sprocket \n(the last one for the ﬁrst level of intake simultaneously the \nﬁrst one for the second level of intake)\nPennlaine\nSprocket\n246\nWe found out that if we only use \nrubber bands, when the upper \nlevel of intake is already ﬁlled up \nwith blocks, which means that the \nupper level will basically not move \nanymore, the rubber bands would \nbe stopped, stretched, and \nsnapped by the motionless \nblocks, leading the lower level of \nintake to also be stuck. \n10/7", "Building Solution\nT o solve this problem, we wrapped this sprocket \nwith the anti-slip mat after our last robot’s \nexperimentation. \nAlso, the anti-slip map supports a consistent \ntouching of the blocks to the mat, or sprocket surface, \nensuring that the ball would be moved up with more \nfriction. \nPennlaine\nSprocket\n247 10/7", "Selecting Solution\nThe motion of the important sprocket must be chained \nto the lower level because:\n1. the sprocket does not move in position. \n2. It is built on the lower level’s angle structure. \nTherefore, if it was chained to the moving-in-height \nupper level, the chains would be stretched out and \nsnap. \nPennlaine\nSprocket\n248 10/13", "Selecting Solution\nAfter the sprocket’s size was decided, based on its \nshaft height, like mentioned, maintaining on a same \nbasis of other shafts of the ﬁrst level and parallel to \nthe mat, we ﬁnished up the upper level’s ground \nsprockets. We could only use small sprockets because:\n1. We had to minimize the robot’s height\n2. If the radius were too large it would interfere with the \nlower level sprockets and intaked blocks\nPennlaine\nSprocket\n249 10/13", "Selecting Solution\nAlthough we all use rubber bands with sprockets like \nwe reasoned before, we decided to use ﬂex wheels for \nthe outake instead of sprockets because:\n1. The blocks are not entirely balls, they consist edges \nthat make its size not consistent, therefore we need \nsome amount of compression\n2. However , the compression should not be too much \nbecause using sprockets with rubber bands would \nmake the outaked blocks come out with less force. \nLittle amount of compression would squeeze the \nblocks out with higher force. \nPennlaine\n250\nSprocket\n10/15", "Building Solution\nAs mentioned before, our last sprocket will ensure a \nconsistent touching of the mat and the ball, creating a \ncurved movement. Therefore, we wanted to add plastic \nboards supporting the mats to be tied on it to create a \ncurved surface. \nWe used 3d printing to test if our CAD drawing was \ncorrect, if so, we would laser cut plastic boards.\nWe continued to revise the board shape. \nPennlaine\nMovement\n251 10/16", "Building Solution\nWhile revising the aforementioned plastic boards, we \nwere also starting to design the roof of the robot, \nwhich would also be plastic boards, as well as adding \nthe whole pneumatics system and electronics.\nPennlaine\nPlastic Boards\n252 10/17", "Identify the problem\nT o design the plastic boards:\n1. decide where the standoffs would be placed since \nsome places have structures like bearings already. \n2. Draw the determined holes\n3. The roof’s wide is 5.5 inches, over 4 already, so the \nlength could only be 4. Therefore, we decided to cut \nthe roof into two halves, making the width less than 4, \nspeciﬁcally 2.75, and the length could be 8 inches. \nThe edges of the boards would have holes to connect \nthem with zip ties.\n4. We decided that we would make the roof hollow \nbecause this would allow us to :\n- see the colors of the blocks inside\n- increase the amount of compression and extension\n5. We then decided to make all our plastic boards \nhollow with triangular designs. \nPennlaine253\nRoof\n10/18", "Design\nThis is the process of developing our roof\nPennlaine\n1 2\n3\n5 6\n4\n254\nRoof\n10/18", "Design Anthony255\nRoof\n10/18", "Design Anthony256\nRoof\n10/18", "Design\nThis is the process of designing the side sectors that \nwould support the mats. \nT esting Solution\nWe 3d printed out this prototype to test out its \ndimensions. \nPennlaine\n1 2\n257\nMat Support\n10/18", "Design\nAfter we 3d printed out this prototype, we hallow \ndesigned it for laser cutting.\nPennlaine\n3 4\n258\nMat Support\n10/18", "Design Anthony\n4\n259\nMat Support\n10/18", "Testing Solution\nAfter ﬁnishing developing the roof, we also made the \naforementioned plastic board for the last sprocket \nhollow with triangular patterns.\nWe also found out that the balls would need more \namount of compression for going up to the second \nlevel, so we added long #117b rubber bands.\nPennlaine\nPlastic Boards\n260\n 10/18", "Identify the problem\nWe had to use a blocking mech for the blocks to not \nfall out when the robot is moving or when the intake is \non:\n1. We need to design a board driven by pneumatics, \nwhich hinders the blocks from falling out when \npistons retracted\n2. It will also act as descoring when pistons extended.  \nThis required it to be a triangular shape to enter the \nlong goal. \n3. Ideally, making it triangular could also help align our \nrobot when at goals. \n4. Along with this, we had to develop space for the \npistons to place. \nPennlaine\nDescorer\n261\n 10/19", "Design Pennlaine\nDescorer\n262\nSince we had to use 3d printing for prototyping ﬁrst, \nwe had to CAD a 3d board, since the descorer would be \nbent. \n10/19", "Design Anthony\nDescorer\n263\n 10/19", "Design Pennlaine\nDescorer\n264\nWe also had to design the piston holding boards for \nboth sides of the bot and test the doable position for \nthe pistons. \n10/19", "Testing Solution\nWe 3d printed the descorer and the piston holders and \ntested out the optimal positions. \nPennlaine\nDescorer\n265\n 10/19", "Design Pennlaine\nDescorer\n266\nAfter determining and testing the speciﬁcations, we \ncould revise and ﬁnalize our plastic design our \ntrianglular hollowing. \n10/19", "Design Anthony\nDescorer\n267\n 10/19", "Design Anthony\nDescorer\n268\n 10/19", "Testing Solution Pennlaine\nDescorer\n269\nWe laser cutted out the boards and tested successfully. \n10/19", "Building Solution\nT o this point, our robot is mostly ﬁnished, what \nremained was autonomous coding, which will be \ndetailed further ahead. We are now ready for next TAI \nVEX event.\nPennlaine\nRobot 2\n270 10/19", "Programming Solution Pennlaine\nButton Mapping\nButton Function Type Implementation\nAxis 2 Left drive Analog T ank drive control\nAxis 3 Right drive Analog T ank drive control\nR1 Intake forward Hold Both intake \nmotors\nR2 Intake forward Hold Both intake \nmotors\nL1 Lower intake \nonly\nHold Fine \nmanipulation\nL2 Lower reverse Hold Ring rejection\nY T oggle shooter Press Pneumatic toggle\nB T riangle blocker Press Pneumatic toggle\nRight Intake extend T oggle Pneumatic toggle\nDown Aligner toggle Press Pneumatic toggle\nProgramming\n271 10/6", "Programming Solution Pennlaine\nT ank Drive\n- Basically the same as previous     \nvoid usercontrol() {\n   task intake(intakeControlTask, 0);\n   task hangTask(hangControlTask, 0);\n   task momogo(momogoTask, 0);\n   task notetask(autonoteTask, 0);\n  \n   // Button bindings\n   Controller1.ButtonY.pressed(shooterSwitch);\n   Controller1.ButtonRight.pressed(intakecylanderon);\n   Controller1.ButtonB.pressed(hang);\n   Controller1.ButtonDown.pressed(alignerSwitch);\n   Controller1.ButtonR1.pressed(shooterOn);\n   Controller1.ButtonR1.pressed(alignerON);\n   Controller1.ButtonR1.released(shooterOff);\n   Controller1.ButtonR1.released(alignerOFF);\n  \n   while (1) {\n       chassis.control_tank(100);  // 100% speed scaling\n   }\n}\nProgramming\n272 10/6", "Programming Solution Pennlaine\nIntake Functions\nCoast mode allows manual back-driving if jammed\nint intakeControlTask() {\n   while (true) {\n       // Priority: L1 > L2 > R1 > R2\n       if (Controller1.ButtonL1.pressing()) {\n           intake.stop(coast);\n           intakedown.spin(forward, 12, volt);  // Lower stage only\n       }\n       else if (Controller1.ButtonL2.pressing()) {\n           intake.stop(coast);\n           intakedown.spin(reverse, 12, volt);  // Lower stage reverse\n       }\n       else if (Controller1.ButtonR1.pressing()) {\n           intake.spin(forward, 12, volt);      // Both stages forward\n           intakedown.spin(forward, 12, volt);\n       }\n       else if (Controller1.ButtonR2.pressing()) {\n           intake.spin(forward, 12, volt);      // Both stages forward\n           intakedown.spin(forward, 12, volt);\n       }\n       else {\n           intake.stop(coast);\n           intakedown.stop(coast);\n       }\n       wait(20, msec);\n   }\n}\nProgramming\n273 10/6", "Programming Solution\n Pennlaine 10/6\nAutonomous Strategy\n274", "Programming Solution Pennlaine\nWe continued our previous intake strategy and started \nto code. \nAs mentioned, we have three main functions to control \nthe robot’s path.\n1. void turn_to_angle(float angle)\n2. chassis.drive_distance(inches, heading, \nDriveVolt, HeadingVolt)\n3. void cos_move_distance_smooth(distance_in, \nangle_deg, turn_maxV, drive_maxV)\nWe have to turn to the desired angle before moving, or \nelse the k values would lead it to run unpredictable \npaths. \nThe main difference between drive_distance and \ncos_move_distance_smooth is that the latter \naccelerates and deccelerates and stops with brake to \nnot let the deceleration continue unpredicatbilly. \n10/8\nProgramming\n275", "Programming Solution Pennlaine\nThis is a demonstration of a snippet of our code:\nWe use drive_distance() for most because it doesn’t \noverheat the motors since it’s using coast for braking. \nWe use cos_move_distance_smooth for smaller \ndistances because it stops with brake precisely and it \nwould be more smooth and slow to prevent small \nfragments and tilting or ﬂiping the robot. \nchassis.turn_to_angle(5); // to long goal\n wait(0.05,sec);\n chassis.drive_distance(12, 5, 6, 6);\n pushCylinder.set(true);\n wait(0.05,sec);\n shooter.set(true);\n wait(0.05,sec);\n cos_move_distance_smooth(4, 5, 6, 6);\n10/20\nProgramming\n276\nProgramming", "Brainstorming \nSkills Strategies Ver.1\nWe also decided to play in skills in TAI VEX HS.\nIn consideration of our design of the robot and to \nalign with the rules of driving skills, we will intake in the \ncenter and score the center goal ﬁrst. We decided to \ngo ﬁnish scoring the two center goals within the ﬁrst \nfourty seconds and use the last twenty seconds for \nclearing one loader and score the balls into the long \ngoal. Furthermore, we will park if we have sufficient \ntime. \nAnthony\n 10/21277", "Brainstorming \nNew Driver Strategies\nIn At the start of the match, the driver immediately \ndrives to the center Triballs and pushes them into the \ncenter goal within the ﬁrst 10–15 seconds. This will give \nour alliance early control of the scoring area and \nprevents opponents from taking the center. From 15 to \n60 seconds, the driver focuses on collecting remaining \nTriballs from the defensive side and quickly adding \nthem to the offensive zone. One important thing that \nwe observed through the past matches is that the \ndriver should avoid chasing single balls too far , \nbecause wasted time reduces total points. \nGreen: Auto\nRed: initial stage\n(10-15 seconds)\nWhite: middle game\n(15-60 seconds)\nBlack:ﬁnal seconds\n(parking)\nAnthony 10/22278", "Brainstorming \nNew Driver Strategies\nIn the last 15–20 seconds, the driver positions the robot \nin the parking zone to park for the 8-point bonus. \nAnthony\n 10/22279", "Project Management Pennlaine 10/24\n2025/10/24 — 60 minutes\nDiscussion topics\nProgress:\nThe event is tomorrow and we have listed out things \nunﬁnished:\n1. Auto skills\na. did not meet our plan and not tuned\n2. Driver practicing\na. we did not have enough time for the driver to \npractice\nb. therefore we also did not mock driver skills \nHowever , we’re better than last time and ready to face \nnext battle\nMeeting  Preparation \n280\nWho is here Goals for today’s meeting\n- Pennlaine\n- Adam\n- Anthony\n- Jefferson\n- Progress before competition\n- Division of work\n- Interview practicing\n", "Competition Analysis\nTAI VEX HS 26\nCecilia281\nMATCH RESULTS\nP3 78181A\n75663A 42 65 10004B\n23083Z\nQ1 92641S\n17022A 50 37 96969W\n23083Z\nQ10 23083Z\n17022J 54 42 92641A\n75663A\nQ12 96969Y\n11645A 98 18 23083Z\n2779A\nQ19 23083Z\n66994T 87 28 92641C\n10004B\nQ24 66994B\n78181A 155 3 23083Z\n75016A\nQ29 23083Z\n1791V 69 21 75016B\n66994D\nQF 3-1 78181A\n17022A 88 56 23083Z\n92641A\n10/27", "Competition Analysis\nMatch P3 was a practice round that went fairly well. Our \nalliance’s strategy worked since one of our opponents \nno-showed. As a result, our alliance took turns applying heavy \ndefense on 78181A. However , 78181A was still able to execute a \nmassive descore near the end of the round. Our alliance won \nnonetheless, but our coach argued that we should have used \nthe practice round to test whether our bot was functioning \nproperly.\nTAI VEX HS 26\nAdam\n282\nREFLECTIONS & \nIMPROVEMENTS\n78181A\n75663A 42 65 10004B\n23083Z\n10/30", "Competition Analysis\nMatch Q1 was an embarrassing experience. Our intake motor \nmalfunctioned because the rubber band was not in place. As a \nresult, our team was unable to score much, while our opponent, \n17022A, a particularly strong team, scored signiﬁcantly even \nthough their alliance was accidentally tripped at the beginning \nof the match.\nAdam\n283\nTAI VEX HS 26\nREFLECTIONS & \nIMPROVEMENTS\n92641S\n17022A 50 37 96969W\n23083Z\n10/30", "Competition Analysis\nMatch Q10 went smoothly overall. Theoretically, our team should \nhave dominated since both our alliance and opponents \nappeared weaker based on our analysis. However , our bot was \nrepeatedly blocked by a pan-shaped bot, and our maneuvering \nwas not very effective. One opponent camped near the long \ngoal, while another effectively gatekept the central goal. We \nrealize we should have a better maneuvering technic.\nAdam\n284\nTAI VEX HS 26\nREFLECTIONS & \nIMPROVEMENTS\n23083Z\n17022J 54 42 92641A\n75663A\n10/30", "Competition Analysis\nMatch Q12 was a disaster. Our opponent applied heavy defense \non our bot throughout the match. Our ally was largely \nineffective, and we missed many blocks while scoring long goals. \nWe need to improve our scoring speed, as our bot is too easily \npushed away by opponents during scoring.\nAdam\n285\nTAI VEX HS 26\nREFLECTIONS & \nIMPROVEMENTS\n96969Y\n11645A 98 18 23083Z\n2779A\n10/30", "Competition Analysis\nMatch Q19 went very well. Our alliance was strong, and their bot \nsinglehandedly secured the long-goal control zone after the \nautonomous phase. Our alliance focused on camping at the \nlong goal to attempt descoring our opponents’ remaining \nblocks, while one opponent did the same. We tried to push them \naway but were unsuccessful due to our low mass. However , we \nsuccessfully scored 4 blocks into the other long goal and \nparked.\nAdam\n286\nTAI VEX HS 26\nREFLECTIONS & \nIMPROVEMENTS\n23083Z\n66994T 87 28 92641C\n10004B\n10/30", "Competition Analysis\nMatch Q24 is unreviewable. Our team was disconnected at the \nbeginning of the match. \nAdam\n287\nTAI VEX HS 26\nREFLECTIONS & \nIMPROVEMENTS\n66994B\n78181A 155 3 23083Z\n75016A\n10/30", "Competition Analysis\nMatch Q29 was absurd. Our bot played defensively while our \nalliance focused on offense by targeting the long goal. Our \nalliance managed to score a considerable number of blocks, \nand our bot successfully blocked the opponent’s wing, allowing \nour alliance to execute a successful descore. Although we won \nby score, our team were disqualiﬁed because we did not hear \nthe head referee’s three-second count while defending against \nthe opponent.\nAdam\n288\nTAI VEX HS 26\nREFLECTIONS & \nIMPROVEMENTS\n23083Z\n1791V 69 21 75016B\n66994D\n10/30", "Competition Analysis\nMatch QF #3-1 , we faced a formidable opponent that ultimately \nplaced second. The key takeaway from this match is the need for \na strong descoring mechanism, as our opponent, 17022A, scored \nan entire tube in the ﬁrst half of the match and we were unable \nto descore it. This directly led to our decision to add a wing \nafter the tournament. Additionally, our scoring speed is slow, \nand the ﬁnal blocks often get stuck. We still have much to \nimprove.\nAdam\n289\nTAI VEX HS 26\nREFLECTIONS & \nIMPROVEMENTS\n78181A\n17022A 88 56 23083Z\n92641A\n10/30", "Competition Analysis\nAnalysis of \nOthers’ Robots\nAdam\n290\nT eam: 78181A\nT ournament: TAI VEX HS 26\nKey Features:\n● High-mobility S-bot design\n● Strong descoring mechanism\n● Fast cycle time\n● Excellent loader-clearing ability\n● \nSummary:\n78181A is a fast, highly efficient \nS-bot with exceptional \ndescoring and loader control, \nan opponent we greatly \nadmire.\n10/30", "Competition Analysis\nAnalysis of \nOthers’ Robots\nAdam\n291\nT eam: 66994B\nT ournament: TAI VEX HS 26\nKey Features:\n● Championship-winning basket bot\n● Extremely strong loader intake (~6 blocks per \nsecond)\n● Dual-sided wings for fast descoring\n● High-capacity basket (~10 blocks)\nStrategy & Performance:\n● Rapid multi-block intake and high-throughput \nscoring\n● Used wings for quick and effective descoring\nSummary:\n66994B is a high-speed, \nhigh-capacity basket bot \nwith powerful intake and \ndescoring, making it the \ndominant robot of the \ntournament.\n10/30", "Competition Analysis\nAnalysis of \nOthers’ Robots\nAdam\n292\nT eam: 66994T\nT ournament: TAI VEX HS 26\nKey Features:\n● High-mobility slope bot design\n● Ruiguan-style slope variation\n● Dual-sided wings for fast descoring\n● Strong driver control\nStrategy & Performance:\n● Executed quick descoring with wings\n● Fast and consistent long-goal scoring\n● \nSummary:\n66994T combines a proven \nslope-bot design with \nexcellent driver control for \nfast descoring and efficient \nlong-goal scoring.\n10/30", "06\nROBOT 2.1\n293", "Project Management Jefferson 11/03\n2025/11/02 — 45 minutes\nMeeting information :\nWe have a new joined team member Benjamin, who will \nbe mainly responsible for programming and aligner.\nWe started to rebuild thie car and ﬁx the problems that \nitoccur during the previous competition. Our leader \ngives each of us a job to do and plan to ﬁnish repairing \nthe car after two weeks. We aalso found out some \nimportant functional parts of robot that other team has \nattach and plays a very important role in each battle. We \ndecide to create these parts to maximize our car’s \nability. \nMeeting Logs\n294\nWho is here Goals for today’s meeting\n- Pennlaine\n- Adam\n- Anthony\n- Jefferson\n- Benjamin\n- New member\n- Improvements\n- Planning\n", "Identify the problem\nAfter discussion and reﬂecting our TAI-VEX event, and \nwe focused on major defects we now needed to \nimprove, proved in the event. \n1. The height of extending piston for the second level \nwasn’t enough, leading to the robot not able to \nefficiently and consistently score into the long goal. \n2. Our robot also had the problem of not able to intake \nblocks immediately at the point which it intake \ntouches it.\n3. We did not have an effective descore mech that was \nable to quickly descore the opponent’s blocks, and \nwe found out this a major obstacle. \n4. We also didn’t have an aligner that was able to help \nus align upon the goals fast enough. \n5. We also weren’t able to cross the parking zone, which \ndecreased our scoring for skills and the parking \nscore in matches. \nPennlaine\nReﬂections\n295 10/30", "Project Management Jefferson 11/2\nBuilding Timeline\n296\nTasks 11/2-\n11/8\n11/9-\n11/15\n11/16-\n11/22\n11/23-\n11/30\n12/1-\n12/7\nShooter Design \n \nIntake Design \nAligner \nDescorer (wing) \nProgramming \nT asks per member:\n- Shooter: Pennlaine, Adam\n- Intake design: Anthony, Jefferson\n- Aligner: Pennlaine, Adam\n- Wing: Benjamin, Adam\n- Programming: Pennlaine\n- Simulator: Anthony\nSAT Prep\nExam Prep", "Building Solution\nAligning to our to-do list, the ﬁrst one was the easiest, \nin which we extend the pistons length using standoffs, \nand because we were using spacers when it retracts, \nthis also allowed it to maintain and not affect the \nprevious height when pistons are retracted\nPennlaine\nShooter Height\n297 11/2", "Identify the problem\nThe second issue is our weak descoring ability. Since it \ninterferes with most of the upper part of the robot, we \nmight as well redesign the intake in the meantime, \nwhich mainly comes from the lower part of the \nstructure. While we were brainstorming away our \ndescore mechanism, we ran into a bunch of problems \nthat kind of clash into each other:\n1. Our previous descore board was neither long \nenough nor high enough. \n2. However , it provided the function of able to impede \nblocks from falling out since the intake is always \nrolling.\n3. The most used mech was \nable to be in use because \ntheir last few outake \nsprockets were individually \ncontrolled by another motor. →\nAnthony\nShooter Design\n298 11/2", "Brainstorming \nTherefore, we have to think of a mech that allows the \nblocks able to spin freely in the shooter while the \nintake was on, but at the same time blocking the \nblocks to prevent them from falling out. \nResearch:\n8889A has a similar design as us, and they exactly \nmeet our problem. \nPennlaine\nShooter Design\n299 11/2", "Brainstorming \n8889A used a unique mech to solve this problem. \nThey used pistons to pull a string, and when the \npistons are extended, the string would be loose \nenough for the blocks to past through and score. \nWhen the pistons are retracted, its tension would \nprevent blocks to pass through and fall out. \nPennlaine\n300\nShooter Design\n11/3", "Design\nWe built a prototype to test our solution: using pistons \nas a stopper. When the pistons are retracted, it pulls \nthe standoff up, right in the middle of the shooter \npathway, creating a physical barrier that keeps the ball \nfrom moving forward.\nThis way, even with the descorer sitting higher , we can \ncontrol the ball ﬂow without everything spilling out.\nPennlaine\nShooter Design\n301\n 11/6", "Selecting Solution\nT o design the perfect shape for the height \nincorporating with the piston extension route. \nWe 3d printed out several prototypes and determine \nthe optimal one. \nPennlaine\nShooter Design\n302\n 11/7", "Testing Solution\nWe transplanted the prototype to our robot and tested \nthe whole system out, we found several aspects that \ncould improve this mech. \nPennlaine\nShooter Design\n303\n 11/7", "Identify the problem\nFrom our 3d printed prototype, we noticed that:\n1. We could extend the board to the upper aluminum \nangle to improve both the plastic boards and the \nshooter system’s stiffness. \n2. Because now we can build a ﬂexible descorer board, \nit needs a limit holding hole to maintain it at the best \nheight for descoring upon the goals. \nPennlaine\nShooter Design\n304\nFlexible \nDescorer\n② →\n①\n11/7", "Selecting Solution Anthony\nShooter Design\n305 11/7\n", "Building Solution\nAfter determining the plastic board shape, we cutted \nout transparent plastic board and replaced the 3d \nprinted boards. \nThis is our ﬁrst CNCed plastic board since we have just \nreceived our new CNC a few days ago!\nAnthony\nShooter Design\n306\n 11/7", "Design\nT o ﬁx the problems, we re-designed the plastic board \nshapes to meet the to requirements. \nWe printed  3 types to ﬁgure out which curve and limit \nheight would suit best. \nAnthony\nShooter Design\n307\n② \n①\n11/7", "Identify the problem\nAnother issue with the intake is that the last sprocket, \nwhich acts both as the ceiling of the intake and the \nﬂoor of the shooter , tends to get stuck. In theory, our \nbot can hold 6 or 7 blocks, 8 if you really push it. But \nafter loading around 4 blocks, everything jams. The \nblocks reach the shooter , the last row stops moving, \nand that ends up freezing all the sprockets below it \nsince everything’s chained together. \nAnthony\nIntake Design\n308\n 11/7", "Brainstorming \nNow, as sprockets are chained together , stopping the \nlast row from moving meant the whole intake has to be \nreimbursed. Effectively cutting the capacity in half, \nwhich is devastating. We can’t even clear a loader now.\nWe’ve tried to ﬁx this issue by:\nIssuing a separate 5.5 watt motor for the last row.  But, \nthis will leave the  shooter will just another 5.5 volt \ninstead of 11. And honestly, that is too risky, because we \ndon’t think the shooter will have enough torque to \nactually ﬁre anything reliably.\nAnthony\nIntake Design\n309 11/7", "Selecting Solution\nWe also need to ﬁx the issue where the intake was \nsitting way too deep inside the bot and couldn’t reach \nblocks near the wall. \nAnthony\nIntake Design\n310\n 11/7", "Testing Solution\nT o make sure that the ﬂex wheel intake would perform \nbetter than our current intake, we did an experiment. \nTEST 1\nT est the intake efficiency under standardized \nconditions. \nT est Procedure\n1. Place the intake prototype upon the loader\n2. Along with our tongue prototype, load 6 blocks from \nthe loader\n3. Start intake and timer simultaneously\n4. Stop timer when ﬁnal block is fully secured\nResults\nAnthony\nIntake Design\n311\nT rial Sprocket Intake Flex Wheel Intake\n1 5.82 3.94\n2 5.67 3.81\n3 5.91 3.76\n4 5.74 3.88\n5 5.85 3.72\n11/7", "Testing Solution\nTEST 1\nAvg Intake Time\nBelow plot shows TEST 1 results:\nAnthony\nIntake Design\n312\nPrototype Avg Time (s)\nSprocket 5.80\nFlex Wheel 3.82\n11/7", "Selecting Solution\nTEST 1\nAnalysis\nThe ﬂex wheel intake demonstrated signiﬁcantly higher \nefficiency by conforming to block surfaces and \nmaintaining continuous contact. This reduced tangling \nand allowed faster , more consistent block acquisition.\nAnthony\nIntake Design\n313\n 11/7", "Testing Solution\nT o make sure that the ﬂex wheel intake would perform \nconsistently on our robot cooperating with sprockets. \nTEST 2\nT est the intake on the robot. \nT est Procedure\n1. Place the intake prototype upon the loader\n2. Feed 6 blocks into the intake\n3. Start intake and timer simultaneously\n4. Stop timer when ﬁnal block is fully secured\nResults\nAnthony\nIntake Design\n314\nT rial Intake → Storage Time\n1 4.21\n2 4.58\n3 4.15\n4 4.42\n5 4.30\n6 4.48\n7 4.25\n8 4.12\n11/7", "Testing Solution\nTEST 2\nAvg Time Spent intaking\nBelow plot shows TEST 2 results:\nAnthony\nIntake Design\n315\nAvg Time (s) Std Dev (Consistency)\n4.34 ±0.15 s\n11/7", "Selecting Solution\nTEST 2\nAnalysis\nThe ﬂex wheel intake performed smoothly and \nincorporated seamlessly with the current chain of \nsprockets. With a low standard deviation indicating \nconsistent and uniform intaking. \nAnthony\nIntake Design\n316\n 11/7", "Building Solution\nBy adding standoffs and a couple extra angle pieces, \nwe 1. swapped the ﬁrst row from sprockets to ﬂex \nwheels, and 2. pushed the whole ﬁrst row of the intake \noutward. \nAnthony\nIntake Design\n317\n 11/7", "Identify the problem\nWe loosened the extending parts to give the intake \nsome give. But that led to another headache: \nnow when the robot intakes, the whole ﬁrst row shoots \nupward and slams into the shooter level, messing \neverything up and throwing the alignment completely \noff.\nAnthony\nIntake Design\n318\n 11/7", "Identify the problem\nInitially, the slope of the plastic was not steep \nenough to let blocks from the loaders slide in \nproperly. It only works if the bot crashes into the \nloader at enough speed, which isn’t exactly ideal. T o \nmake things worse, the tongue now interferes with \nour new intake in a couple of ways:\n1. We plan to add an aligner(blue), and the bottom part \nof the plastic(green) will most likely get stuck there. \n2. Even if it somehow doesn’t block the aligner , after \nfully tucking in(orange), it stop the ﬁrst row of intake \nfrom lifting up, which in turn effectively shuts it down.\nAnthony\nIntake Design\n319 11/10", "Building Solution\nWe rebuilt the tongue \n1. using longer angle bars, and \n2. added a limit so the plastic doesn’t wedge itself into \nthe gap between the intake and shooter anymore. \nIt now stops right before touching the plastic on the \nside of the shooter , which keeps it from blocking the \nintake. We also \n1. shortened the side angle bars, and \n2. shifted them forward. \nThe whole thing became way more rigid, reacted faster , \nand stopped wobbling around like before. It also put \nless stress on the joints, so the tongue isn’t trying to \npry itself apart every time we intake.\nAnthony\nTongue\n320 11/10", "Building Solution\nFor our new descorer , we switched things up and \nused two angle bars instead of the old setup where \nwe just bent the plastic. The reach is deﬁnitely better \nnow, but honestly it still isn’t hitting the height we \nactually need. The limit needs to sit higher so it can \npush the balls cleanly when the mechanism is at rest. \nThe problem can be solved\nby extending the \nplastics on the \nsides upwards.\nAnthony\nDescorer\n321 11/12", "Building Solution\nSince VRC regulations don’t allow any 3D-printed parts, \nwe eventually had to swap our original design for the  \nplastic version. The main change we made was \nbending the front end of the descorer so it could sit \ncloser to the blocks and make more contact during a \npush. This tweak was meant to help the descorer \n“catch” the ball more reliably instead of just tapping it \nwith a thin edge. In theory, the wider contact area \nshould make it easier to guide the ball out of the goal \nwithout the piece slipping off or glancing past it. \nAnthony\nDescorer\n322 11/12", "Identify the problem\nFollowing the previous TAI-VEX event, we are \ndeveloping a front-mounted aligner to solve the critical \nproblem of inaccurate long-goal alignment. This will \ndramatically improve our scoring consistency.\nThe proposed aligner features:\n● Function: A passive guide for precise and repeatable \nrobot positioning.\n● Mounting: Front-mounted on the robot, above the \nintake system.\n● Form: Still needs testing to effectively funnel the \nrobot into the correct scoring position.\nBy implementing this solution, we aim to eliminate \nalignment errors and increase our scoring efficiency.\nBenjamin\n 11/12\nAligner\n323", "Brainstorming \nWe are inspired by 3150V’s aligner placement and form, \nas their robot looks highly identical to ours. Therefore, \nwe are starting to design one with a trapezoid shape \nlike theirs. \nBenjamin 11/12\nAligner\n324\n", "Design\nThe initial trapezoid-shaped aligner was designed with \ninspiration from 3150V , but we also thought that we \ncould test different shapes. \nBenjamin\n 11/12\nAligner\n325", "Selecting Solution\n Anthony 11/12\nAligner\n326\nAligner_1 Isometric", "Design\nFor the second aligner prototype, we implemented a \ndesign with matching dents to actively engage the \ngoal's triangular base.\n● Intended Function: T o slide perfectly into the goal's \nframe for a secure and accurate lock.\n● Active Alignment: A new hole and structural guide \nthat positively \"locks\" into the goal's bottom triangle \nfor precise alignment.\n● Solved Interference: The tongue mechanism was \nmodiﬁed (by moving and shrinking the front plastics) \nto create necessary clearance.\nBenjamin\n 11/15327\nAligner", "Selecting Solution\n Anthony 11/15328\nAligner\nAligner_2 Isometric", "Testing Solution\nAfter cutting out both prototypes, we did a small \nexperiment:\nTEST 1\nT o determine which aligner design is more efficient in \nimproving the robot’s scoring position accuracy.\nT est Procedure\n1. Start the robot from the parking zone \n2. Start Timer\n3. Driver control to long goal\n4. Stop timer when robot positioned\nBenjamin 11/15329\nAligner\nT rial Prototype A Prototype B\n1 4.82 3.91\n2 4.95 3.78\n3 4.71 3.85\n4 4.88 3.73\n5 4.90 3.80\n6 5.03 3.92\n7 4.76 3.74\n8 4.92 3.87", "Testing Solution\nTEST 1\nAverage aligning time:\nBelow plot shows TEST 1 results:\nBenjamin 11/15330\nAligner\nPrototype Avg Time (s)\nA 4.88\nB 3.82\n", "Identify the problem\nTEST 1\nAligner Prototype 1 had several fatal problems:\n1. The design had zero tolerance for error. It required a \nperfectly straight approach, which was impractical \nduring match conditions.\n2. Even minor misalignments caused the mechanism to \nfail, drastically reducing its effectiveness.\nDue to this fundamental issue, we have decided to \nchoose Prototype 2.\nBenjamin 11/15331\nAligner", "Selecting Solution\nTEST 1\nAnalysis\nPrototype 2 showed signiﬁcantly faster aligning, about \n21% faster. Proving that valleys could help the robot slip \ninto the right position. \nDecision\nHowever , we could still improve in the prototype 2’s \nshape and dimension to make it more efficient. \nBenjamin 11/15332\nAligner\n", "Selecting Solution\nFor the third and ﬁnal version of the aligner , we \nevolved the static dents into an angled, funnel-like \nguide. This new design features sloped surfaces that \nautomatically correct the robot's position as it \napproaches the goal. By channeling the robot into \nthe correct alignment, the guide ensures it \"locks\" \nprecisely into the triangular structure. \nBenjamin 11/17333\nAligner\n", "Building Solution\n Anthony 11/17334\nAligner\nAligner_3 Isometric", "Project Management Pennlaine 11/15\n2025/11/13 — 60 minutes\nMeeting information:\nOur ﬁrst time management isn’t well organized. We \nhaven’t ﬁnish improving our robot. We gave a better time \nschedule and emphasized each team members jobs. \nDue to a little conﬂict in our team. We think our team is \nlacking beneﬁcial conversation. We stayed together to \ndiscuss about our conﬂict and solutions. \nOur solution was that we need to list out everything that \nhappened by the end of the day, and the team leader \nwould then reply what each member would do the next \nday based on who will go tomorrow. And then again \neach would respond what they solved and what new \nproblems happened. \nMeeting Team Organization\n335\nWho is here Goals for today’s meeting\n- Pennlaine\n- Adam\n- Anthony\n- Jefferson\n- Team organization\n- Practice Schedule\n", "Brainstorming \nOur initial descoring strategy was passive; a \nfront-mounted mechanism would displace balls only \nwhen the robot forcefully collided with the long goal. \nHowever , we recognized this was inconsistent and \nlimited. Inspired by the strategic functionality of team \n8889A's wing mechanism, we decided to develop an \nactive descoring system. \nBenjamin 11/18\nWing\n336\nWING Demonstration \nfrom team 8889A", "Selecting Solution\nOur new design will feature a dedicated wing that \ncan be driven into the goal's channel on demand, \nallowing us to:\n1. swipe blocks out at a time of our choosing, and\n2. place the blocks at the control bonus section, \nwhich both add a signiﬁcant strategic function to our \nrobot.\nBenjamin 11/18\nWing\n337", "Testing Solution\nOur initial wing design was a simple assembly: an \nangled plate attached to a piston, supported by a \nstandoff. However , testing revealed signiﬁcant ﬂaws. \nBenjamin 11/18338\nWing\n", "Identify the problem\n1. The structure lacked the necessary stiffness, and \n2. the piston's ﬁt created excessive friction against the \ngoal channel, hindering smooth operation.\nBenjamin 11/19339\nWing\n② ①", "Selecting Solution\nT o resolve these issues, we implemented a \ncomprehensive redesign:\n● Revised Mounting: We changed the entire \nwing-mounting format to improve structural rigidity.\n● New Actuation: We switched to a single-acting piston.\nBenjamin\n● Positive Return: We added a \nrubber band to provide a \nreliable return force when \nthe piston is released, \nensuring the wing retracts \ncompletely.\n11/19340\nWing", "Building Solution\nFollowing an iterative process of prototyping and \ntesting, we successfully engineered a ﬁnal wing \nstructure that perfectly balances strength, function, \nand form factor.\nThe breakthrough came from building the wing's core \nframework not from simple plates, but from a \ncomposite structure using standoffs (copper pillars) \nand C-channels. This approach provided the ideal \ncombination of properties:\n1. the C-channel's cross-section provides inherent \ntorsional strength, resisting the twisting forces \nexperienced during descoring. When strategically \nconnected and triangulated with solid standoffs, it \ncreates a lightweight yet incredibly rigid frame that \ndoes not ﬂex during use.\nBenjamin 11/19341\nWing", "Building Solution Benjamin\n3.  The structure naturally \nincorporates strong, multi-point \nattachment locations for the \nsingle-acting pneumatic \ncylinder , eliminating the ﬂex \ninherent in a simple \nangled-plate mount.\n11/19342\nWing\n2.  By carefully sizing the outer dimensions of this \nC-channel and standoff frame, we created a structure \nthat ﬁts precisely within the goal channel. This \"snug but \nsmooth\" ﬁt ensures it can slide in reliably without the \nexcessive friction that bound our previous piston-based \ndesign.\n", "Brainstorming \nT o solve the problem of unable to either double park \nor cross the parking zone, we thought of some \npossible solutions:\n1. add side plastic boards to aid\n2. change the lateral stiffness structure that connects \nthe chassis underneath\na. the structure was built to enable double parking, \nbut it is just at the point for half of the parking \nzone so it actually knocks our opponent off from \nparking\nb.we decided to change its position to on top of \nthe chassis, but because of the ramp, there is \nnearly no space. \nPennlaine 11/20\nParking\n343", "Building Solution Pennlaine 11/24\nWe did eventually ﬁnd a place to set our lateral \nstiffness structure, but we had to move our inertia \nposition.  Also, this created a risk that the shaft \nbetween a sprocket wheel and the structure are too \nclose together.\nSince we moved our intake forward out, we had to also \nreposition the sprockets to connect them smoothly, so \nwe also had to reposition the gold channel for motors.  \nRobot 2\n344", "Project Management Jefferson 12/12\nBuilding Timeline\n345\nTasks 12/8-\n12/14\n12/15-\n12/21\nDescorer (wing) \nProgramming \nT asks per member:\n- Wing: Benjamin, Adam\n- Programming: Pennlaine\n- Simulator: Anthony\n- Driver Practice: Adam", "Identify the problem Anthony 12/13\nAlthough we previously said that the wing is the ﬁnal \nproduct, the wing actually broke off several times \nduring friendly matches due to repeatedly crashing \ninto long goals. \nWing\n346", "Building Solution Anthony 12/13\nTherefore, in case it breaks again during competitions, \nwe made some backups that are identical and able to \neasily and quickly rebuild and ﬁx.\nWing\n347", "Identify the problem Anthony 12/13\nIn the update of V2.1, <SG10> is updated to that \nreaching and anchoring into the goal is prohibited, \ntherefore we need to change our previous design of \nusing a rubber bumper.\nWing\n348\nPROs CONs\nThe rubber bumper had a \nlarger volume, giving us \nmore momentum while \npushing the blocks\nIt might get us stuck within \nthe enclosed section of the \nlong goal if we retract our \npistons there, which causes \nus to illegally anchor inside \nthe goal. \n", "Brainstorming Anthony 12/13\nAs opposed to before, instead of using the bumper \nto push the blocks forward, we opted for a \nscythe-like tip instead, inspired by Killer Instinct \n6842K.\nWing\n349\n", "Building Solution Anthony 12/13\nWe curved a screw into the state you see here. This \nchange makes it much more easier to dip it in the \ngoal, as its thinner and it follows the trajectory of \nwhere it falls. \nWing\n350", "Identify the problem Anthony 12/13\nAnother problem we encountered was that the \nsuction part of the edge of the long goal effectively \nshuts down the possibility of our wing advancing \ninto the inner parts of the goal. So we ﬁrst used a \nplastic board to stand in the way, bouncing the wing \nup from the energy of the crash, but upon thinking \nwe realized we have used up all the available plastic \nparts, we switched to a angle bar instead.\nWing\n351", "Identify the problem Benjamin 12/12\nJust 5 days before the PAS T ournament…\nOur robot broke apart…\n \n352\nBroke….", "Identify the problem Benjamin 12/12\nIt was an ordinary practice day, progressing as \nplanned, until our driver , Adam, suddenly shouted, \n“Huh, it broke?!”\nWhen we inspected the robot more closely, we \ndiscovered that a critical structural component—the \nmain supporting ramp structure—had failed. Two angle \nplates had split in half, compromising the integrity of \nthe robot. \n353\nBroke….\n", "Building Solution Benjamin 12/12\nWe immediately halted all practice and shifted our \nfocus to repairs. The damaged parts were replaced \nwith stronger components, and additional \nreinforcement was added to increase overall stiffness. \nThis incident highlighted a weakness in our design and \nemphasized the importance of structural durability. \nAfter completing the repairs, we carefully tested the \nrobot, hoping the improvements would prevent similar \nfailures in the future.\nBroke…\n354", "Building Solution Benjamin 12/13\nAfter testing the repaired robot, it felt as though the \nrobot had been completely renewed, with improved \nstrength and performance. Although the failure was \nunexpected and concerning, it ultimately led to \nmeaningful improvements in our design. The \nexperience was a close call, but it resulted in positive \noutcomes and a stronger , more reliable robot.\nFixed!\nAdam was doing \nsome magic to help \nus repair…\n355\n", "Building Solution Pennlaine\nPneumatic System Diagram:\nPneumatic System\nWing\nT ongue\nShooter Block\nInput\nT Fittings \n276-8638\nT Fittings \n276-8638\nT Fittings \n276-8638\nT Fittings \n276-8638\nShut-off Valve \n276-8755\nSingle Acting \nSolenoid Double Acting Solenoid\n276-8752\nT Fittings \n276-8638\nT Fittings \n276-8638\n50mm Stroke \n276-8644\n50mm Stroke \n276-8644\n50mm Stroke \n276-8644\n75mm Stroke \n276-8646\n75mm Stroke \n276-8646\n75mm Stroke \n276-8646\n356 12/16", "Building Solution Pennlaine357\nEngineering Drawing for \nRobot 2\n12/17\n", "Building Solution Benjamin 12/17\nFinal 23083Z’s Robot 2:\nRobot 2 \n358\nIt’s even short \nenough to place \nunder the seats on \nthe T aipei MRT!", "Building Solution Pennlaine\nWe have 4 pneumatic sets, each representing a \nfunction of the robot. \n1. T ongue:\na. double acting\nb.75mm\nc. 1 piston\n2. Shooter:\na. single acting (double acting solenoid but one end \nplugged in)\nb.50mm\nc. 2 pistons\n3. Blocker:\na. double solenoid\nb.75mm\nc. 2 pistons\n4. Wing:\na. single acting\nb.50mm\nc. 1 piston\n359\nPneumatic System\n12/25", "Programming Solution Pennlaine\nWe decided that our autonomous takes up too much of \nour time, so that we can’t prepare for other things, \ntherefore we either need to improve its accuracy or \nﬁnd a method to write code with less testing/at home.\nBecause I was designing clothes logos using Inkscape, \nI thought of using x and y positions to improve the \naccuracy. All I needed was one proportional to real life \nmap that could give me X and Y positions. \n360\nProgramming\n11/25", "Programming Solution Pennlaine\nAs we wished to still use PID functions of JR-T emplate, \nwe wanted to maintain chassis.turn_to_angle() and \nchassis.drive_distance() functions. \nThis was more basic than we thought, we don’t need to \nrebuild, recalculate a recording system of x and y \npositions. Instead, we just used the distance = √(dx² + \ndy²) formula to calculate moving distance, while \nturning to the point ﬁrst so that the PID won’t make it \nrun an unpredictable path. \n11/25\nvoid driveToXY(double targetX, double targetY, double maxV, double turnMaxV) {\n   // ========== STEP 1: Calculate target heading (theta) ==========\n   double dx = targetX - robot_pose.x;\n   double dy = targetY - robot_pose.y;\n   double targetHeading = std::atan2(dx, -dy) * 180.0 / PI;\n  \n   // Normalize to 0-360\n   while(targetHeading < 0.0) targetHeading += 360.0;\n   while(targetHeading >= 360.0) targetHeading -= 360.0;\n      \n   // ========== STEP 2: Turn to face target heading ==========\n   chassis.turn_to_angle(targetHeading);\n   vex ::wait(50, vex::msec);  // Small delay after turning\n  \n   // ========== STEP 3: Drive straight to target ==========\n   // Calculate distance to target\n   double distance = std::sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy);\n  \n   // Use chassis.drive_distance to drive straight while maintaining heading\n   // This will maintain the targetHeading we just turned to\n   chassis.drive_distance(distance, targetHeading, maxV, turnMaxV);\n}\nProgramming\n361\nProgramming", "Programming Solution Pennlaine\nAt times we wanted the robot to move in a desired \nheading, and we only want to restrict its x or y position, \nthe other position would be based on the heading, we \nused driveToXAtHeading(). It moves the robot to a \nspeciﬁc X coordinate while maintaining a desired \nheading angle. This is useful when the robot needs to \napproach a goal or obstacle at a speciﬁc angle.\nTherefore we needed to calculate arctan(heading) and \nget the other unknown position. \n11/25\nvoid driveToXAtHeading(double targetX, double targetHeading, double maxV, double turnMaxV) {  \n   double dx = targetX - robot_pose.x;\n   double heading_rad = targetHeading * PI / 180.0;\n  \n   // Handle vertical headings (90° or 270°) where tan is undefined\n   if(std::fabs(std::cos(heading_rad)) < 0.001) {\n       // Heading is approximately 90° or 270° (moving purely in Y direction)\n       // In this case, just drive straight at the heading\n       double distance = std::fabs(dx);  // Use a reasonable distance\n       chassis.turn_to_angle(targetHeading);\n       vex ::wait(50, vex::msec);\n       chassis.drive_distance(distance, targetHeading, maxV, turnMaxV);\n       return;\n   }\n  \n   // Calculate target Y coordinate\n   double dy = -dx / std::tan(heading_rad);\n   double targetY = robot_pose.y + dy;\n  \n   // Now drive to the calculated X,Y coordinate\n   driveToXY(targetX, targetY, maxV, turnMaxV);\n}\nProgramming\n362\nProgramming", "Programming Solution Pennlaine\nFor the actual implementation, we drew our car with \nour actual width and length, and at every point we \nneeded to make a turn or stop, there is an dot, the dot \nwould have the x and y positions, which should be in \ninches, similar to real life. \nI used several points to adjust and calibrate so that \nthe map is closest to real life. \n12/4\nProgramming\n363\nProgramming", "Programming Solution Pennlaine\nThis allowed us to write code at home, and plan out \nour paths, so that the testing time would be minimized. \nWe still used drive distance for unsure movements like \nmoving forward, because it would be complicated if we \nhad to set the coordinates. But no matter how much \nwe (drive_distance) at, we can still go to the next \naccurate position not needing to change any later \ncodes. This is the implementation example:\n12/4\n// Set initial pose: X, Y, and heading (0° = up/forward, 90° = right, \n180° = down, 270° = left)\n set_robot_pose(86, 116.3, 0.0); // Robot starts facing up/forward (0°)\n intakedown.spin(forward, 10, volt);\n // ========== PHASE 1: Drive to intake position ==========\n // driveToXY automatically calculates and turns to face the target\n wait(0.05, sec);\n driveToXY(103, 105, 8.0, 6.0);\n wait(0.05, sec);\n intake.spin(forward, 10, volt);\n // ========== PHASE 2: Score 3 blocks ==========\n chassis.turn_to_angle(315);\n wait(0.05, sec);\n driveToXY(93.5, 93.5, 8.0, 6.0); // Approach slowly\n wait(0.2, sec);\n chassis.drive_distance(15, 315, 8.0, 6.0); // Drive to low goal\nProgramming\n364\nProgramming", "Programming Solution Pennlaine\nOur ﬁrst autonomous path is scoring into 2 goals, \nideally 4 into the middle goal, and 3 from the loader \ninto the long goal. \nThis would be more strategic if our alliance can only \nscore into one goal, so that we can get AWP for scoring \ninto 3 goals. \n12/6\nProgramming\n365\nProgramming", "Programming Solution Pennlaine\nOur other autonomous path is developed for the long \ngoal control zone, in which we will ideally score 9 into \none long goal. This can be better if our alliance can \nscore 2 goals, in order for us to as well get AWP .\n12/9\nProgramming\n366\nProgramming", "Programming Solution Pennlaine\nOur autonomous skills path is slightly different from \nour driver skills, and is expected to have a lower score \ndue to the difficulty of parking and clearing parking \nzones. \n12/11\nProgramming\n367\nProgramming", "Programming Solution Pennlaine\nT o summarize our strategy, we load from all 4 loaders \nand score into the long goals, aiming to get both long \ngoal control zone bonus. Because of the ﬁeld set up, in \norder to guarantee control zone bonus, we shall load \nand score into a long goal from the same open end, \ntherefore both long goals should be red bonus. \nAnd ﬁnally the robot moves to the parking zone and \nclears the parking zone to gain 20 points. \n12/11\nProgramming\n368\nProgramming", "Programming Solution\nSimulator\nAnthony 12/14\nT o further improve our driver’s skills, we’ve updated \nour simulator after an Signature event on 12/26. We \nbasically re-did the whole thing.\nFirst off, we re-designed how the size of everything \nis calculated.\nfunction fit() {\n      const reserved = 420, minW = 800, minH = 600;\n      canvas.width = Math.max(minW, window.innerWidth - \nreserved);\n      canvas.height = Math.max(minH, window.innerHeight - \n24);\n      canvas.style.width = canvas.width + 'px';\n      canvas.style.height = canvas.height + 'px';\nconst reserved = 420 is to reserve space on the right \nside for the control panel. minW and minH ensures \ngame elements remain above a certain size and \nremains constant.\n369", "Programming Solution Anthony 12/14\nSo the code “resizes” multiple times every second \nduring a window drag. The loop goes as follows: \nIt calculates the new dimensions.\ncanvas.width = Math.max(minW, window.innerWidth \n- reserved);\nIt then recenters the playing ﬁeld.\nFIELD_MARGIN_H = ( canvas.width - fieldWidth) / \n2;\nIt at last rebuilds everything from every single thing.\nrebuildFieldObjects();\nA sneak peak of the function:\nfunction rebuildFieldObjects() {\n      const fieldWidth = TILE_SIZE * \nFIELD_WIDTH_TILES;\n      const fieldHeight = TILE_SIZE * \nFIELD_HEIGHT_TILES;\n      BALL_R = TILE_SIZE / 12;\n      BALL_DIAM = BALL_R * 2;\n      robot1.length = TILE_SIZE * ( 2 / 3);\n  //......etc370\nSimulator", "Programming Solution Anthony 12/15\nFor the ball physics, we have to do something really \ncreative, as there isn’t a box for circles. If you get what I \nmean. We fetched the approximation of the circle and \nuse little boxes to build a circle. Like how calculus \ncalculates an objects volume, but reverse.\n                Circle               Approximated Square\n        ┌─────────┐                ┌─────────┐\n        │    ● ● ● ●      │                │     ■ ■ ■ ■      │\n        │   ● ● ● ● ●    │                │    ■ ■ ■ ■ ■    │\n        │  ● ● ● ● ● ●  │       →      │  ■ ■ ■ ■ ■ ■   │\n        │   ● ● ● ● ●    │                │    ■ ■ ■ ■ ■    │\n        │    ● ● ● ●      │                │     ■ ■ ■ ■      │\n        └─────────┘                └─────────┘\nIt turns a weird square-circle calculation into a much \nsimpler square-square check. While not \nmathematically perfect, the approximation saves me a \nlot of time and also provides enough accuracy for the \nengine to make it work.\n371\nSimulator", "Programming Solution Anthony 12/15\nPractically speaking, it enhances computational \nefficiency as I don’t need to code a speciﬁc \nsquare-to-circle method to handle such situations \nwhen its just square-square. Furthermore, the blocks \nare relatively small, paired up with the fast speed of \nbots, the errors are usually minimal, or go unnoticed.\nfunction circleObbPenetration(circle, obb) {\n      // Convert circle to OBB for collision \ndetection\n      const circleObb = { cx: circle.x, cy: \ncircle.y, hx: circle.r, hy: circle.r, angle: 0 \n};\n      return obbObbPenetration(circleObb, obb);\n    } \nWe also added an indicator to let players better \nvisualize what balls they picked up.\n372\nSimulator", "Programming Solution Anthony 12/15\nWe also updated our driving physics:\nfunction updateRobot(robot, dt) {\n      const targetVl = robot.leftPower * \nrobot.maxSpeed;\n      const targetVr = robot.rightPower * \nrobot.maxSpeed;\n      const v = ( targetVl + targetVr) / 2;\n      const omega = ( targetVr - targetVl) / \n(robot.width);\n      const vxTarget = v * \nMath.cos(robot.angle);\n      const vyTarget = v * \nMath.sin(robot.angle);\n      robot.vx += (vxTarget - robot.vx) * \nrobot.linearAccel * dt;\n      robot.vy += (vyTarget - robot.vy) * \nrobot.linearAccel * dt;\n      robot.angularVel += (omega - \nrobot.angularVel) * robot.angAccel * dt;\n      robot.x += robot.vx * dt;\n      robot.y += robot.vy * dt;\n      robot.angle += robot.angularVel * dt;\n    }\n373\nSimulator", "Programming Solution Anthony 12/15\nBecause our robot is a C-bot, it has to lift its upper \nstructure to score long goals, so that functionality was \nadded. We also ﬁxed an issue where lifted C-bots could \nincorrectly pass through long goals.\nif (goalSet === longGoals) {\n        if (!robot.isLifted) return;\n        const padding = 15;\n        for (const g of goalSet) {\n          const goalObb = aabbToObb(g);\n          const triggerObb = { cx: goalObb.cx, \ncy: goalObb.cy, hx: goalObb.hx + padding, hy: \ngoalObb.hy + padding, angle: 0 };\n        } \n        return;\n      } \n374\nSimulator", "Programming Solution Anthony 12/15\nVISUAL OVERHAULS!!!\nIf you can’t tell by now, I did some visual overhauls to \nmake the game look prettier. It took surprisingly more \ntime than I had anticipated. Some of the hardest ones \nto do would be the gradient and the glowing edges.\nconst pulse = Math.sin(Date.now() / 200) * 0.2 \n+ 0.8;\n      ctx.beginPath();\n      ctx.arc(0, 0, robot.length * 0.7, 0, \nMath.PI * 2);\n      ctx.strokeStyle = `rgba(255, 255, 0, \n${0.4 * pulse})`;\n      ctx.lineWidth = 2;\n      ctx.stroke();\nThis segment of the code \nprocesses the rim pulses.\nIt allows the edge of the bot\nto glow and pulse, give it a\nmore lively feel. Is it useful?\nNo. Does it serve a function?\nNo. But is it cool? Hell yes.\n375\nSimulator", "Brainstorming \nSkills Strategies Ver.2\nConsidering that it is highly difficult to get the middle \ngoals bonus, we decided to give up that score. \nWe emphasize the bonus points for long goals. We \nshould make sure that we park and clear the red \nparking zone because that would make a 20 points \ndifference, but our robot can’t efficiently clear the 6 \nblocks at once, it would take too much time. Therefore \nwe start driver skills as intaking about 3 blue blocks \nand then connecting to the ﬁrst loader. \nAnthony 12/16\n376", "Brainstorming \nAs shown in the ﬁgure below, we start from the left side \nof the red parking zone, try to clear half the blocks and \npick them up in order to utilize them later. In an ideal \nsituation, we should clear half the zone and pick them \nup.\nAnthony 12/16\n377\nSkills Strategies Ver.2", "Brainstorming \n3 blue pieces already in the bot. After loading from the \nloader , the bot should hold 9 pieces total, 6 blues \nfollowed by 3 reds. Score them in that order into the \nlong goal to secure the blue control bonus.\nAnthony 12/16\n378\nSkills Strategies Ver.2", "Brainstorming \nCrossing the ﬁeld horizontally, we approach the \nsecond long goal. After loading all six blocks, we score \nonly the three red blocks, saving the three blue blocks \nfor the next control zone.\nAnthony 12/16\n379\nSkills Strategies Ver.2", "Brainstorming \nAfter crossing, we don’t load the 3 blues. Theres a \nmistake in the picture below. Instead, we try to fully \nload the loader with the 3 blues already in there, \nadding up to 9 in total, and score. Blues should reach \nthe control zone with no problem in theory.\nAnthony 12/16\n380\nSkills Strategies Ver.2", "Brainstorming \nAfter scoring the last goal, we turn back to parking. \nThere should be at least 3 or more blocks still in there, \nso after clearing that out we should be able to park.\nAnthony 12/16\n381\nSkills Strategies Ver.2", "Brainstorming \nIdeally, the score would be: 4 cleared loaders (4*5=20), 2 \nﬁlled control zones (2*5=10), 24 blocks scored (24*1=24), 1 \ncleared parked zone (1*5=5), and a parked bot (1*15=15). \nSo the scores would tally up to be 20+10+24+5+15=74.\nAnthony 12/16\n382\nSkills Strategies Ver.2", "Competition Analysis\nCreate Award\nAnthony 1/2\nAs for VEX competition itself, we luckily struck gold  \nduring the PAS Signature Event and ended up taking \nhome the Create Award. While unexpected, the \nrecognition validated our creative and deliberate \napproach to this season. \nOn the topic of this season, it is a grind. Push Back \nis simple yet demands immaculate precision and \nconsistency. Making us feel like we can’t even manage \nsimple tasks at times. Some people on our team even \nleft mid-season. It was that gruelling. \nWhen progress is slow it is easy to focus only on the \ndifficulty of things. Which, inevitably, leads to \ndiscourse among ourselves. Despite it all, receiving \nthe Create Award reminded us why we began this \nseason, to pursue creative engineering solutions, and \nencouraged us to continue improving as a team.\n383\n", "Competition Analysis\nPAS Signature\nCecilia384\nQ4 66994T\n23083Z 106 11 44448C\n2813X\nQ21 67000A\n85301Z 14 88 85301V\n23083Z\nQ40 50922S\n4139B 19 50 23083Z15383A\nQ55 4139C\n23083Z 31 105 96969W66994B\nQ79 85301W\n936D 3 109 23083Z17022A\nQ97 66799X\n23083Z 31 39 4253S\n1002X\nQ114 23083Z\n2779A 45 28 23083Y\n2813Z\nQ140 2769B\n23083Z 43 64 4253W\n92641C\nR16 \n#8-1\n4253Z\n2053V 82 15 13176A\n23083Z\nMATCH RESULTS\n1/5", "Competition Analysis\nPAS Signature \nOne of our opponents was unable to move from the \nstart of the match, which gave our alliance a \nsigniﬁcant advantage. This allowed us to secure the \nwin more easily. Our alliance partner , 66994T, \ndemonstrated strong wing play by defending the \nleft-side long goal in the ﬁnal moments of the match, \nteaching us the importance of correctly timing match \nloads.\nAdam385\n23083Z\n66994T 106 11 44448C\n2813X\n1/5\n", "Competition Analysis\nPAS Signature \nWe had a strong alliance partner who controlled one \nside of the long goal for most of the match. On our \nside, we initially struggled to regain control of the long \ngoal control zone, but we were eventually able to \nretake it by approaching from the opponents’ side of \nthe ﬁeld. The main drawback of our performance was \nthat we were unable to successfully complete a double \npark with our partner’s double park mechanism.\nAdam386\n67000A\n85301Z 14 88 85301V\n23083Z\n1/5\n", "Competition Analysis\nPAS Signature \nOur opponents controlled the long goal for most of \nthe match. Although our team brieﬂy lost momentum, \nwe executed a strong recovery near the end by scoring \nthree balls, regaining the control zone, and removing \nour opponents’ blocks through a solid wing play.\nAdam387\n50922S\n4139B 19 50 15383A\n23083Z\n1/5\n", "Competition Analysis\nPAS Signature \nWe entered this match knowing we were facing the \neventual tournament champion, 66994B, along with \nanother strong opponent. Although our alliance \npartner lost connection partway through the match, \nwe were able to secure the autonomous bonus by \nalmost completing a solo AWP at the start. Overall, \ndespite the loss, this was a match we felt positive \nabout due to our strong autonomous performance.\nAdam388\n23083Z\n4139C 31 105 96969W\n66994B\n1/7\n", "Competition Analysis\nPAS Signature \nQualiﬁcation Match 79 was a textbook example of \neffective alliance coordination. Our alliance \nmaintained control for the entire match. We began by \nfocusing on the center goals while our alliance partner \nsecured their side of the long goal. We then switched \nsides and successfully defended the long goal, \nallowing our alliance to establish control over all \ncontrol zones and secure another victory.\nAdam389\n85301W\n936D 3 109 23083Z\n17022A\n1/7\n", "Competition Analysis\nPAS Signature \nAdam390\n23083Z\n2779A 45 28 23083Y\n2813Z\n1/7\nIn Qualiﬁcation Match 114, we executed a starvation \nstrategy. From the start of the match, after securing \nour own control zone, we focused on defending our \nopponents for most of the match. This prevented them \nfrom scoring into the long goal, while we occasionally \nadded blocks to the center goals to increase our \nadvantage. We concluded the match by parking our \nrobot, securingthe the win.", "Competition Analysis\nPAS Signature \nAdam391\n23083Z\n2769B 43 64 4253W\n92641C\n1/7\nIn our ﬁnal qualiﬁcation match, we faced a formidable \nopponent. We secured the center goal control bonus \nand made strong wing plays throughout the match. \nHowever , in the very last second, our opponents took \nback control of the long-goal control zone by a strong \nwing play. At the same time, our alliance partner’s side \nof the long goal was ﬁlled with opposing blocks.\n", "Competition Analysis\nPAS Signature \nAdam392\n4253Z\n2053V 82 15 13176A\n23083Z\n1/7\nIn this match, we were unable to regain control of the \ncenter goals, which are a key strength of our robot. \nOur opponents used strong center-goal descoring \nand, after gaining control of the long-goal control \nzone, applied heavy defense against us. In addition, \nour chassis experienced overheating and we ran out of \nblocks to score early in the match. Despite the loss, it \nwas a valuable match that provided important lessons \nfor future improvement.\n", "07\nRobot 2.2\n39\n3393", "Identify the problem\nWing\nBecause our robot is low enough to pass under the \nlong goals, installing a wing is more difficult:\n1. A typical wing would be easily damaged by \nopponents at that height.\n2. Due to the narrower design of the upper shooter , \nour wing must be signiﬁcantly longer than others \n(15-hole angles) in order to swing efficiently into the \nlong goals.\nStandard wing (98601Y Atlantis)\nAdam 1/7394\n", "Selecting Solution\nWing\nUnpressurized\nAdam 1/7395\nUltimately, we decided to use a single acting 50mm \nstroke pneumatic cylinder with a rubber band, \ndirecting its rod and hook to the lowest possible point. \nThis allows us to pass under the long goals while \nmaintaining strong wing performance.\nMaximum reach\nGoing under long goals", "Identify the problem\nWing\nsliding surface that allows the wing \nto pass through the ramp\nAdam 1/7396\nBecause the wing’s height depends on the surface it \ncontacts, we identiﬁed a risk that the wing could \nbecome caught on the side ramps of the long goals. ", "Building Solution\nWing\nAdam 1/7397\nT o mitigate this issue:\n1. we added a low-friction sliding surface that allows \nthe wing to pass smoothly through the ramp \nopening.\n2. we incorporated wing aligners that mechanically \nengage with the metal frame and acrylic panels of \nthe long goal. These aligners help guide the wing \ninto position, improving consistency and reducing \nthe driver’s alignment effort during matches.\nWing aligner", "Identify the problem\nDriver\nThe driver’s main goal is to control the ﬁeld by pushing \nballs into the center goal and long goal while \ndefending their scored balls.\nUnlike Driver Skills, each scored ball would earn 3 \npoints, controlling the long goal would earn 10 points, \ntop center goal for 8 points, and bottom center goal \nfor 6 points. \nAnthony\n   1/7398", "Brainstorming \nGame Strategies\nAnthony 1/9\nWe have 3 ways to approach the match.\n1. 1. We rush the mirror opposition as people tend to \nwant to score bonus.\n2. 2. We play offense score-wise and try to score and \ndefend the middle goals.\n3. 3. We play fully defensive and preserve the balls we \nscored in auto.\n399", "Brainstorming \nGame Strategies\nAnthony 1/9\nEarly Game Route 1:\n1. This works best against lightweight bots or bots \nthat have a slower scoring mechanic.\n2. Utilizing good mobility for these attacks is often \nunexpected and invokes a direct response from the \nopposition, breaking protocol. Essentially a cc.\n400\n", "Brainstorming \nGame Strategies\nAnthony 1/9\nEarly Game Route 1:\n3. Does not work well against heavy bots and fast \nscorers.\n4. Leaving your post means the opposition can \ndescore your blocks altogether.\n401\n", "Brainstorming \nGame Strategies\nAnthony 1/9\nEarly Game Route 2:\n1. Prioritizes the middle goals, which offer a \ncombined potential of 35 points.\n2. If opponents push balls toward your side during \nauto, we currently have zero counterplay options.\n402", "Brainstorming \nGame Strategies\nAnthony 1/9\n403\nRoute 3:\n 1. T o preserve the balls already scored \nduring the autonomous period.", "Brainstorming \nGame Strategies\nAnthony 1/9\nMid Game:\n1. Generally opt for heavy middle defense\n2. If an ally is collapsed on by two enemies, we can \nuse our agility to help them or counter-attack by \nscoring on the opposite side of the map.\n404", "Brainstorming \nGame Strategies\nAnthony 1/9\nLate Game:\n1. Maintaining a hold on the center of the map is  \nimportant as teams would want to score 1 for bonus.\n2. One park is worth less than three scored blocks, \nplus the fact that we can’t double park, we don’t park.\n405", "Brainstorming \nSkills Strategies Ver.3\nCecilia 1/9\n1. Start from the parking zone. Drive straight and \nintake ① (the red block at the upper left corner)\n2. T urn the car 90° counterclockwise\n3. Drive to farther center goal, score 2 red blocks +2\n406", "Brainstorming Cecilia 1/9\n4. drive to the loader at the left upper corner , turn while \nmoving\n5. drive forward and intake 3 blue + 3 red blocks\n6. drive around the farther long goal (don’t change \ndirection)\n7 . turn 180°\n+5\n407\nSkills Strategies Ver.3", "Brainstorming Cecilia 1/9\n8. score 3 blue + 3 red\n9. drive forward to loader at the right farther corner and \nintake 3 red + 3 blue\n+2\n+5\n408\nSkills Strategies Ver.3", "Brainstorming Cecilia 1/9\n10. drive backward to farther long goal\n11. score 3 red + 3 blue\n12. drive to the blue parking place\n13. drive through while intaking 6 red (at least 4)\n+6 +5\n+5\n409\nSkills Strategies Ver.3", "Brainstorming Cecilia 1/9\n14. drive backward until the car touches the parking zone \nin order to calibrate its location\n15. Intake ② (red block)\n16. T urn to the right direction\n17 . drive to the center upper goal. Score 7 red blocks. \n+5\n+10\n410\nSkills Strategies Ver.3", "Brainstorming Cecilia 1/9\n18. Drive to the loader at the closer right corner. T urn to \nthe right direction while driving.\n19. Intake 3 blue + 3 red\n20. Drive around the closer long goal\n21. turn 180° so the back faces the goal\n+5\n411\nSkills Strategies Ver.3", "Brainstorming Cecilia 1/9\n22. score 3 blue + 3 red\n23. drive backwards to loader at the closer left corner and \nintake 3 red + 3 blue\n+6\n+5\n412\nSkills Strategies Ver.3", "Brainstorming Cecilia\n24. Drive backwards to return to the long goal\n25. Score 3 red + 3 blue into the closer long goal\n26. T urn and drive to intake 2 red + 2 blue for the closer \nleft\n27 .  Score them into the lower center goal\n+6 +5\n+4\n413\nSkills Strategies Ver.3\n1/9", "Brainstorming Cecilia\n28. Drive toward the parking zone\n29. Park. Clear the parking zone by intaking/pushing out \nblocks in the park zone +5 +15\n414\nSkills Strategies Ver.3\n1/9", "08\nTeam\n Mangement\n415", "Team Management\nClub Website\nPennlaine 1/2\nT o make a record of our club and team on the open \nsource web, we built our own website. It acts as a site \nto:\n1. Record and demonstrate our team photos\n2. Record and demonstrate our robots and projects\n3. Help us host mock tournaments for our teams\nhttps://23083z.com/\n416\n", "Team Management\nClub Website\nPennlaine 1/2\nWe put the photos we love the most and the ones that \nwe hope to review in our later years on the website.\n417\n", "Team Management\nClub Website\nPennlaine 1/2\nWe previously use VEX’s official site for practice \nmatches internally, but it was hard and inconvenient to \nuse: https://education.vex.com/leaderboard/v5\nProblems of this site:\n1. It can’t record a series of matches, only individual \nones\n2. It doesn’t have a count down timer for \nmatches, only a stopwatch, so we have to \ncorporate with the V5RC Hub app\n3. It also doesn’t have the alarms for matches\n418\n", "Team Management\nClub Website\nPennlaine 1/2\nTherefore, we developed our own API of hosting mock \ntournaments. The site features:\n1. Computer calculated match schedules randomly \nassigning teams with alliances. \n2. Countdown Timer for both Autonomous and Driver. \n3. T eam rankings simulating official calculation \nmethods with WPs, SPs, and APs. \n419\n", "Team Management\nClub Budget\nPennlaine 1/3\nWe have very much limit with our budget plan every \nyear , since our school gives us minimal support:\n1. We collected $640,000 NTD this season\n2. The registration plan is about $50,000 NTD each \nteam, which makes the registration fee 24% of our \nbudget.\n3. Materials and T ools (T op Priority): We already have \nmost of the tools in our lab. We are planning for \n$300,000 NTD for abundant materials for all 3 teams \nover the season, making up 46% budget.\n4. T eam Image and Promotion: Uniforms, Pit \ndecorations, and giveaways should be made with \nleisure time and extra money. \n5. Event-Related Fees: Since not every participant \nwould participate in every event, the fee might vary. \n420", "Team Management\nPractice\nWego Robotics Practice Sessions \n(T eam 23083Z and T eam 23083Y): \nBefore each tournament or competition, our team \nwill participate in multiple practice sessions with the \nother Wego Robotics team (23083Y). During each \npractice, we will analyze our strategies, identify errors, \nand make adjustments to improve overall performance. \nThese sessions allow us to evaluate both teams’ \nstrengths and weaknesses and share feedback to \nsupport one another’s development. By practicing \ntogether , we aim to strengthen our driver skills, \nimprove teamwork, and reﬁne our competitive \nstrategies.\nBenjamin 12/15\n421", "Team Management\nPractice\nWego Robotics Practice Sessions \n(T eam 23083Z and T eam 23083Y): \nIn times when we only have another team that can \npractice with us, in order to mock real time matches, we \nuse a special setup for practicing. Our problem is that \nwith only two bots on the ﬁeld, either both robots are \nfocusing on scoring or both are defending each other. \nTherefore, we set up the ﬁeld with what we call “a hellish \nbeginning.” For the driver who is mainly practicing now, \nwe will setup the ﬁeld as if the opposing alliance has a \nbig win. For example, this pictures shows a setup for the \nred alliance to practice and try to still win, and the blue \nalliance driver should act as a defender who already \nhas advantage. \nBenjamin 12/15\n422", "Team Management\nTeam Impact\nPennlaine 1/2\nOur club technically does not have a coach, we self \nlearn all by ourselves and receive knowledge from our \nseniors, our experiences, and researching online. \nAt the same time, since most of our clubmates are \nnewbies to VEX, our team plays an important role (also \nspends a lot of time in) teaching our clubmates. We \nalso try to make sure that the whole process is ﬁnished \nby students themselves, and we only help with (1) \nsolving their questions, (2) providing them essential \ninformation, and (3) keep an eye on them to make sure \ntheir on task/on schedule. \nWe are also newbies in teaching students, and we also \nlearn a lot while working and studying different robot \nstructures. While having our own time pressure, we do \nour best to help our other teams whenever they need \nhelp, and we feel proud when they win awards. \n23083G 23083Y\n423", "Team Management\nTeam Impact\nPennlaine\nWe also helped a newly formed rookie team 69471A \nwhich contains students from our friend FRC team we \nmentioned before. They started a middle school VEX \nteam this year and we also helped them. \n1/3\n424", "Team Management\nClub Organization\nPennlaine 1/3\nOur school is a highly academic school, which \nprioritizes classes and grades ﬁrst. We only have 2 \nhours of club time a week. We have to make up practice \ntime ourselves, while also keeping up our grades, or \nelse we would be banned from practicing VEX. \n425\n", "Team Management\nClub Organization\nPennlaine 1/3\nOne of our biggest obstacle is the school. Although we \nhave our own robotics lab, we have to apply at least \nthe day before we are going to use it. And if we \nsometimes forget to apply, we can’t use it the next day. \nOur supervisor keeps our keys, so anytime we need to \nenter the lab she has to permit. We aren’t allowed to go \nto the lab during class times, short break times, or \nwhen we did not apply. Also, the school opens at 8:00 \nA.M. to 4:00 P.M. on the weekends. All these factors \ncause a lot of inconvenience, it compresses our \npracticing time. so time management becomes \nessential. \nWe even built the lab with little of  the school’s help!\n426\n", "Team Management\nClub Organization\nPennlaine 1/3\nBecause of this, we often move our ﬁeld around, trying \nto ﬁnd a shelter to practice:\n427\n", "Team Management\nClub Organization\nPennlaine 1/3\nWe have a very limited budget. Our money comes \npurely from the students. The VEX participant fee is \n40,000NTD each season, which start in May each year. \nOur main cost comes from:\n● VEX materials\n● T ools and instruments\n● T eam and Events registration fee\n● Transportation, Accommodation, Food Fee during \nevents\n● T eam uniforms\n● Pitlane Decorations\n● T eam giveaways\n428", "Team Management\nClub Budget\nPennlaine 1/3\nWe have very much limit with our budget plan every \nyear , since our school gives us minimal support:\n1. We collected $640,000 NTD this season\n2. The registration plan is about $50,000 NTD each \nteam, which makes the registration fee 24% of our \nbudget.\n3. Materials and T ools (T op Priority): We already have \nmost of the tools in our lab. We are planning for \n$300,000 NTD for abundant materials for all 3 teams \nover the season, making up 46% budget.\n4. T eam Image and Promotion: Uniforms, Pit \ndecorations, and giveaways should be made with \nleisure time and extra money. \n5. Event-Related Fees: Since not every participant \nwould participate in every event, the fee might vary. \n429", "Team Management\nClub Budget\nPennlaine 1/3\nCategory % Amount \n(NTD)\nExpalnation\nVEX Materials 50% 320,000 Priority\nTools & \nInstruments\n20% 128,000 Only \nessentials\nTravel / Food / \nHotel — — — — Paid before \neach event\nUniforms 5% 32,000 Bargain!\nPit \nDecorations\n1.5% 9,600 Bargain!\nGiveaways 0.5% 3,200 Stickers/Souv\neniers\nEmergency \nFund\n23% 147,200\nBigger safety \nnet\n430", "Team Management\nVEX Materials\nPennlaine 1/3\nWe actually have a diversed source of VEX official \nmaterials:\n1. We have a robotics teacher who has teached us \nEV3 for years, therefore he initially helps us with \nbuying VEX materials from T aiwan’s importer, \nwhich he has known for years. \na. We list out what we need in a table and he \nhelps us order with the importer\nb. We can’t really know the price until the moment \nwe pay for it, after we get the products\nc. We normally just multiply the price from the \nofficial site by 140% to get an idea of the total\n431\n", "Team Management\nVEX Materials\nPennlaine 1/3\nHowever , we noticed that this method has several \nproblems:\n1. It’s expensive and we don’t know the price!\n2. The shipping time is unpredictable\nWe learned from team 78181A Genesis that they directly \nbuy from VEX’s official site, which is surprisingly not \ntaking a lot of time. \nThe ﬁrst thing we bought was this year’s ﬁeld mats, \nsince the old mats’ dimensions were slightly smaller , \nmaking the ﬁeld not accurate!\n432\n", "Team Management\nVEX Materials\nPennlaine 1/3\n2. Therefore, we also started to order parts from the \nVEX official website, and it only takes about 1 week \nshipping time! We renewed our ﬁeld with the \naccurate mats., ﬁnally. \nAssembling our new ﬁeld and new mats!\n433\n", "Team Management\nVEX Materials\nPennlaine 1/3\n3. We also bought parts from RoboSource, since we \nlike the anodized standoffs, and lightweight plastic \nscrews and nuts the site provides. \n434\n", "Design\n3D Printing\nPennlaine 1/10\nBambu Lab P1S\nIntroduction of Bambu Lab P1S\n● The Bambu Lab P1S is a CoreXY high-speed 3D \nprinter designed for speed, reliability, and \nmulti-material printing. \n● It has an enclosed chamber for better temperature \ncontrol and print quality for high-heat materials.\nUsage\n● Although 3dp materials can’t be used on the robot \nfor any purpose, we use it for controller extension \nparts. \n● We don’t have CNC through most of the season, so \nwe use 3dp for plastic boards testing, then ask one \nof our FRC friend team to laser cut it for us. \nhttps://makerworld.com/en/models/1616053-vex-v5-scuff-tntn#proﬁleId-17053\n41\n435", "Design\nComputer Numerical \nControl (CNC)\nPennlaine 1/10\n3 Axis CNC 3040T Router Engraver\nWith limited budget, we asked our school if they would \nlike to sponsor us for a CNC. After long discussion and \nbargaining, our school agreed to install the 3 Axis CNC \n3040T Router Engraver for us with a price of $120,000 \nNTD.\n436", "Design\n3D Printing\nPennlaine 1/10\nBambu Lab P1S\nWe decided to use 3D Printing for giveaways, because:\n1. relatively cheap\n2. high versatility and easy to change\nWe used Blender to create giveaways that are designed \nas license plate and push back blocks. We will make \nthem into keychains.\n437", "Design\nAcrylic Bending \nMachine\nPennlaine 1/10\nManual Acrylic Bending Machine\nFor customizing plastic boards, we bought a Acrylic \nBending Machine so that we can bend, since a lot of \nteams use upward bent plastic boards as descorer \nmech. \n438", "Start Date: \nThank you \nfor reading \n"];
